2026-09-09 12:05  aleidinger

	* doc/html/history.html, frontend/get_audio.c,
	  frontend/get_audio.h, frontend/lame_main.c,
	  test/unit/test_get_audio_float.c:
	  Report input samples clipped on read
	  
	  The encoder takes integer samples, so a floating point sample
	  beyond full
	  scale cannot be carried in and the reader clamps it. The
	  conversion happens
	  before any scaling the user asks for, so no later stage can
	  recover it.
	  
	  The reader counts those samples and the frontend reports the
	  count at the end
	  of the encode, so the file can be re-encoded at a lower input
	  level. This is
	  separate from the clipping report beside it, which measures the
	  decoded output
	  at the current gain and needs decoding on the fly.
	  
	  A sample of exactly full scale is clamped but not counted: -1.0
	  becomes the
	  smallest integer either way. -32768/32768 is exactly -1.0, so a
	  16 bit
	  recording touching full scale becomes a floating point file made
	  of such
	  samples, and counting them would report an intact file as
	  clipped.
	  
	  The unit test asserts the count rises for clipped samples and for
	  nothing
	  else, including at that boundary.
	  
	  SF bug #512.

2026-09-09 12:04  aleidinger

	* doc/html/history.html, frontend/get_audio.c,
	  maintainer/gen-usage.py, make_changelog.sh, misc/lameid3.pl,
	  misc/mk_mp3.sh, misc/mugeco.sh, test/cvscheck.sh,
	  test/unit/Makefile.am, test/unit/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/test_get_audio_float.c:
	  Encode floating point input at the level the file stores it
	  
	  The frontend reads floating point audio through two readers,
	  libsndfile
	  and the LAME internal reader.
	  
	  The libsndfile path asked libsndfile to convert the samples to
	  integers, which
	  scales a file by its own peak. A quiet file was encoded at full
	  scale, and the
	  same file encoded differently depending on how LAME was
	  configured.
	  
	  Both readers now take the samples as they are and convert them in
	  one place.
	  Files holding double precision samples take the same path.
	  
	  The unit test pins that conversion in both file reading backends.
	  What it
	  cannot see is a whole file being scaled before it is called, so
	  the harness
	  that compares two builds end to end has the check for that.
	  
	  SF bug #510.

