	  Build the internal documentation without warnings
	  
	  The internal Doxygen configuration reported nine warnings. None
	  of them
	  stopped anything: the reference is generated on request, and a
	  page that
	  comes out wrong costs nothing at build time.
	  
	  Three blocks of disabled code in the ACM codec are commented out
	  with
	  /* ... */ and each holds another /* inside it. A compiler ends
	  such a
	  block at the first */ and is content; Doxygen counts the inner
	  one and
	  then runs off the end of the file looking for a close that is not
	  there.
	  Those blocks now sit behind #if 0, which says what is meant of
	  disabled
	  code and does not depend on how a scanner counts comment markers.
	  The
	  conversion has to close the inner comment properly, because
	  inside #if 0
	  it is a comment again rather than text within one. A fourth block
	  of the
	  same shape goes with them.
	  
	  The rest is documentation that no longer describes what it sits
	  on: two
	  parameter lists that name one argument and leave the other, a
	  parameter
	  whose name was taken from the first word of its own description,
	  blocks
	  left behind on declarations that are commented out, whose
	  parameter lists
	  were then carried down to the next function that still exists,
	  and an
	  angle-bracketed placeholder in prose that was read as markup.
	  
	  Rendering the two document sets before and after gives the same
	  pages and
	  the same entries, so this is the warnings and nothing else.

