	* doc/html/history.html, frontend/parse.c:
	  Say so when a tag value cannot be converted
	  
	  Tag text given on the command line is converted from the locale's
	  character
	  set into the one the tag needs. That conversion stops at the
	  first character
	  it cannot produce, and its result was discarded, so a value the
	  locale
	  cannot express was written cut off at that point and nothing was
	  said. On a
	  machine whose locale is plain ASCII - which is what a process
	  gets when the
	  requested locale is not installed - that is every accented
	  character.
	  
	  What could be converted is still written, since a transliterating
	  conversion
	  is what was asked for, and the value that lost part of itself is
	  now named
	  along with the encoding it could not be written in. A run that
	  converts
	  cleanly says nothing, and --silent covers these messages as it
	  covers the
	  other tag ones.
	  
	  The three converters had the same body apart from two encoding
	  names and, in
	  one of them, a byte-order mark to leave room for, so they are now
	  one. Three
	  copies were three places for the answer to go unread.

