2026-08-26 14:36  aleidinger

	* config.h.in, configMS.h, configure, configure.ac,
	  doc/html/history.html, libmp3lame/util.c:
	  Arm the debug build's floating point exceptions where the
	  platform can
	  
	  --enable-debug arranges for floating point exceptions to raise a
	  signal
	  rather than pass silently. On Unix that was compiled wherever the
	  operating
	  system is Linux, which is two guesses at once: it reaches for a
	  header that
	  belongs to GNU libc, and it uses the mask names of the x87 unit.
	  Android and
	  musl are Linux and have neither, so the build stopped there; a
	  Linux machine
	  with no x87 stops on the second half for its own reason.
	  
	  Configure now asks for what the code uses. The first choice is
	  feenableexcept(), which needs no architecture names and covers
	  the systems
	  the x87 path never reached - the BSDs, and Linux on anything
	  without an x87.
	  Where it is absent the x87 path remains, and where the header
	  arrives without
	  the mask names, neither is compiled and a debug build does
	  without the aid.
	  
	  Whether an armed exception then traps at all is the hardware's
	  business: an
	  AArch64 implementation need not offer trapping exceptions, and
	  there the call
	  succeeds and execution carries on.
	  
	  Nothing here is compiled into a normal build.

2026-08-26 14:36  aleidinger

	* doc/html/history.html, libmp3lame/mpglib_interface.c,
	  libmp3lame/util.h, test/unit/Makefile.am, test/unit/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/test_decode_scale.c:
	  Restore clipping detection and accurate ReplayGain
	  
	  Both --clipdetect and --replaygain-accurate work by decoding the
	  stream as
	  it is encoded and measuring the result. That measurement reads
	  the decoded
	  samples in the encoder's own scale, where full scale is 32768;
	  the decoding
	  library hands them back normalised to one. The two have disagreed
	  since the
	  decoder was replaced, by a factor of 32768, and neither option
	  has worked
	  since: --clipdetect could not report clipping under any
	  circumstances, and
	  --replaygain-accurate returned the same figure for every input -
	  a figure
	  so far outside the range the tag can express that LAME warned
	  about it.
	  --replaygain-fast, the default, analyses the input rather than
	  the decoded
	  output and was never affected.
	  
	  The samples are brought into the encoder's scale where the
	  decoder's output
	  crosses into it, rather than at each of the places that read
	  them, so the
	  function's declaration and its callers agree again and a future
	  caller gets
	  what the declaration promises. It now says what that scale is.
	  
	  The encoded audio does not move. What changes is the ReplayGain
	  information
	  in the LAME tag, and only when one of the two options is given.
	  
	  A unit test covers the scale, which nothing did before - it is
	  not visible
	  in any signature, which is why the change went unnoticed. The
	  test asks the
	  question without naming the factor: the same stream decoded
	  through the
	  clipped path, whose scale its type fixes, must give the same peak
	  as the
	  unclipped one.

2026-08-26 14:35  aleidinger

	* doc/html/detailed.html, doc/html/history.html, doc/man/lame.1,
	  frontend/lame_main.c, frontend/main.h, frontend/parse.c:
	  Optionally write ReplayGain to the ID3v2 tag as well
	  
	  LAME computes the ReplayGain figures and stores them in its own
	  tag. The
	  new --replaygain-id3v2 writes them additionally as the two
	  user-defined
	  ID3v2 text frames REPLAYGAIN_TRACK_GAIN and
	  REPLAYGAIN_TRACK_PEAK. It is
	  off by default.
	  
	  The figures are not known until the audio has been encoded, and
	  the tag is
	  written before it, so the frames are reserved with placeholders
	  and
	  overwritten in place afterwards - the same way the LAME tag frame
	  is
	  filled in. The values take the form the ReplayGain specification
	  gives,
	  which is not a fixed width, so the reservation asks for a few
	  bytes of
	  ID3v2 padding that the finished frames give back. That the
	  rewritten tag
	  really is the length of the one already in the file is checked
	  before
	  anything is written, and a mismatch leaves the file alone rather
	  than
	  overwriting the audio behind the tag.
	  
	  The peak is a by-product of decoding while encoding, so its frame
	  is
	  written only where something measured one. Three situations
	  produce no
	  frames at all, each saying so rather than leaving a placeholder
	  behind: an
	  output that cannot be written to twice, an input whose own tag is
	  passed
	  through unchanged, and ReplayGain analysis switched off.

2026-08-26 14:32  aleidinger

	* config.h.in, configMS.h, configure, configure.ac,
	  doc/html/detailed.html, doc/html/history.html, doc/man/lame.1,
	  frontend/lame_main.c, frontend/lametime.c, frontend/lametime.h,
	  frontend/main.h, frontend/parse.c, test/unit/Makefile.am,
	  test/unit/Makefile.in, test/unit/test_file_times.c:
	  Optionally give the output file the date and time of the input
	  file
	  
	  --preserve-modtime copies the input file's modification and
	  access times
	  onto the output, when encoding and when decoding alike. It is off
	  by
	  default. Without it every file a re-encoded collection produces
	  carries
	  the moment of the run, and a library that was ordered by date no
	  longer
	  is.
	  
	  The times are read before the input is opened, because reading a
	  file
	  updates its access time: taken at the end, what would be
	  preserved is
	  the moment of the encode rather than anything the input had.
	  Creation
	  time is left alone - there is no portable way to set one.
	  
	  Standard input and standard output have no file to take the times
	  from
	  or to give them to, so a pipe on either side is passed over
	  silently. A
	  named file that could not be stamped is reported.

2026-08-23 08:45  aleidinger

	* doc/html/history.html, frontend/brhist.c, frontend/brhist.h,
	  frontend/lame_main.c:
	  Show the bitrate the encoded audio actually has
	  
	  While encoding, the figure under the histogram was the average of
	  the rates
	  the individual frames were coded at, weighted by how many frames
	  used each.
	  That is not the bitrate of the file: the frames differ in size
	  from their
	  nominal rate, and the difference, while small, is exactly what
	  the display is
	  asked about.
	  
	  The frontend writes every one of those bytes and can divide them
	  by their
	  playing time. The tag frame is left out of the total, since it
	  carries
	  metadata rather than audio and is not among the frames counted
	  alongside it -
	  with it in, a constant excess would be reported, largest on the
	  shortest
	  files. Constant bitrate encodes therefore still display their
	  nominal rate.
	  
	  Reported by Elio Blanca, patch ticket #65. The patch there
	  reaches the same
	  figure through a new library entry point over an internal
	  counter; the counter
	  has not yet seen the tag frame or the flush when the display is
	  drawn, and the
	  number the frontend already holds needs no addition to the
	  library's
	  interface.

2026-08-23 08:45  aleidinger

	* configure, configure.ac, doc/html/history.html,
	  libmp3lame/Makefile.am, libmp3lame/Makefile.in:
	  Build where the toolchain cannot restrict a library's exported
	  symbols
	  
	  libmp3lame asks libtool to link the shared library against an
	  export list, so
	  that only its documented interface leaves the object. libtool
	  does that with
	  whatever the host provides, and a toolchain that provides nothing
	  of the kind
	  could not build the library at all - reported for tcc, which
	  links executables
	  itself and rejects the option outright.
	  
	  configure now asks libtool whether such a link works and leaves
	  the export
	  list out where it does not, saying so on the console and in its
	  summary,
	  because a library built that way exports everything it defines.
	  The question
	  goes to libtool rather than to the compiler, since the mechanism
	  differs
	  between hosts and testing for any one of them would discard the
	  export list
	  where it works perfectly by another.
	  
	  Reported by Elio Blanca on the lame-dev list.

2026-08-23 08:44  aleidinger

	* config.h.in, configure, configure.ac, doc/html/history.html,
	  frontend/main.c, frontend/main.h, frontend/parse.c:
	  Build the command line tools where the C library has no strnlen
	  
	  The frontend calls strnlen in six places and nothing tested for
	  it, so a
	  system predating C11 and POSIX.1-2008 could not link the tools -
	  reported on
	  Mac OS X 10.5.8. configure now asks for the function, and the
	  frontend
	  supplies its own where the answer is no.
	  
	  The replacement is a bounded scan rather than a call to strlen.
	  Each of these
	  call sites reads a buffer that need not contain a terminator at
	  all, which is
	  why they were written bounded, and substituting strlen would put
	  back the
	  unbounded read they exist to avoid.
	  
	  Reported by Robert Kausch on the lame-dev list.

2026-08-23 08:43  aleidinger

	* configure, configure.ac:
	  Reject a compiler flag the toolchain cannot link with
	  
	  The optimisation and security hardening flags configure adds were
	  chosen by
	  compiling a trivial program with each one. Several of them make
	  the compiler
	  emit references to a support library, so a toolchain can accept
	  the flag,
	  pass the probe, and then fail to link anything built with it -
	  reported on
	  Haiku, where the stack protector needs a library the system does
	  not ship.
	  The probe now links, which is the question it was meant to ask.
	  
	  The flags chosen on a working toolchain do not change; the
	  linker-flag probe
	  beside it already worked this way.
	  
	  Reported by Robert Kausch on the lame-dev list.

2026-08-22 11:57  aleidinger

	* libmp3lame/VbrTag.c:
	  Correct the Xing/Info header layout comment
	  
	  The block describing the tag listed the table of contents before
	  the frame
	  count and the stream size, put the LAME tag at 20 bytes, and
	  totalled to a
	  figure matching neither of the two defines it sat above. The
	  writer emits
	  those fields the other way round: the six it counts are
	  VBRHEADERSIZE's 120
	  bytes, and the 36-byte LAME tag that follows brings
	  LAMEHEADERSIZE to 156.
	  Each define now carries its own sum.
	  
	  Reported by Vincent HO-SUNE on SourceForge (patch #100). The same
	  ticket also
	  sets a further bit in the header flags word. That part is not
	  taken here.
	  Nothing is known to read it - the LAME tag is found by its
	  version string,
	  here and everywhere else - while setting it would alter the
	  header of every
	  file the encoder writes, on Xing headers as well as on the Info
	  headers it is
	  named for.

2026-08-22 11:57  aleidinger

	* TODO, doc/html/history.html, libmp3lame/util.c,
	  test/unit/Makefile.am, test/unit/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/test_resample_decision.c:
	  Resample whenever the input sample rate differs from the output
	  rate
	  
	  Input and output sample rates that agreed to about four digits
	  were treated
	  as equal and the audio was passed through unresampled. That was a
	  guard
	  rather than a policy: a resampling ratio very close to one used
	  to be rounded
	  to an exact integer, which chose the wrong filter length and let
	  the window
	  index run past the precomputed filter table. The rounding was
	  corrected in
	  2015; sample rates are integers, so equality is the whole
	  question. The TODO
	  entry that recorded the workaround goes with it, and that list is
	  a bullet
	  list now, so retiring an entry no longer renumbers the rest.
	  
	  A unit test states the decision over rates that straddle the
	  width of the old
	  guard, including the two that sat on its edges, and encodes
	  across a
	  one-hertz mismatch end to end, so the path that used to fault is
	  exercised on
	  every run.
	  
	  Reported, with a patch, by Riley Gabriel on SourceForge (patch
	  #101).

2026-08-22 11:56  aleidinger

	* doc/html/history.html, frontend/lame_main.c,
	  test/unit/Makefile.am, test/unit/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/test_exit_status.sh:
	  Report a failed read through the exit status
	  
	  When an input file cannot be read to the end, the frontend's
	  readers say so,
	  but the encoding and decoding loops both ended on the same
	  condition as a
	  clean end of input. A truncated output file was written, nothing
	  was printed,
	  and the process reported success, so a caller in a script had no
	  way to tell a
	  finished encode from an abandoned one.
	  
	  Both loops now stop where the read failed, name the failure, and
	  exit
	  non-zero - the status a missing input file has always produced.
	  Output for
	  input that reads to the end is unchanged, byte for byte.
	  
	  A unit test drives the built frontend over a deliberately damaged
	  file on both
	  paths, with a clean input and a missing one beside it, so it can
	  pass neither
	  on a build where everything fails nor on one where nothing does.
	  
	  Reported, with a patch for the encoding case, by Van de Bugger on
	  SourceForge
	  (bug #513).

