	* ACM/AEncodeProperties.cpp, test/clients/acm_test.cpp,
	  test/clients/ctest.h, test/clients/dshow_test.cpp:
	  Check the shape of the ACM's configuration file before reading it
	  
	  The codec looks up a root element and a child of it in the file
	  it keeps
	  beside itself, and then uses both without asking whether either
	  was found.
	  A file that parses as XML but is shaped some other way makes one
	  of them a
	  null pointer. Five of the six places that read the file did this;
	  the sixth
	  already checked, and is what the other five now look like.
	  
	  The file is hand-editable and is exactly what a failed write
	  truncates, so
	  this is not a malformed-input case so much as an ordinary one. It
	  matters
	  more than the same mistake elsewhere would: this class runs
	  inside a driver
	  that Windows has loaded into some other application's process, so
	  following
	  a null pointer takes that application down rather than the codec.
	  
	  A file that cannot be read at all already leaves the defaults in
	  place, and
	  that is now what a file that reads but is not ours does too.
	  
	  The tests cover three shapes of it, and check the values
	  afterwards rather
	  than only that nothing crashed - a build that stopped opening the
	  file would
	  otherwise pass. Their output is now unbuffered, because a test
	  that can take
	  its own process down loses a buffered transcript exactly when it
	  is needed.

