2026-08-16 10:31  aleidinger

	* ACM/ACMStream.cpp:
	  Compare the ACM's compression ratio without flooring it first
	  
	  In smart-output mode the codec derives an output sample rate from
	  the
	  compression ratio and then refuses any conversion whose
	  destination does
	  not match it, so this ratio decides which conversions are
	  possible at
	  all. Every operand of the expression is an integer, so the
	  division
	  truncated before the comparison and a ratio of 13.7 was treated
	  as 13.
	  
	  Three source and destination pairs are affected, all of them at
	  that
	  ratio: 24 kHz stereo at 56 kbps, 48 kHz mono at 56 kbps and 48
	  kHz
	  stereo at 112 kbps. Each of them now downsamples as intended
	  instead of
	  keeping the source rate.

2026-08-16 10:31  aleidinger

	* ACM/AEncodeProperties.cpp:
	  Keep the ACM's smart-output ratio as written in its configuration
	  file
	  
	  The smart-output threshold is a compression ratio, and the ratios
	  it is
	  compared against are fractional - 13.78 for 44.1 kHz stereo at
	  128 kbps
	  against a default threshold of 15 - so the fractional part is
	  where the
	  setting does its work. It was stored as a whole number, which
	  left a
	  configured 2.5 working for the session and floored on the next
	  save.
	  
	  Both directions now go through the C locale rather than the
	  host's. The
	  codec is loaded into whatever application asked for it, so the
	  decimal
	  separator in force is that application's: without this, a ratio
	  one host
	  wrote would read back wrong on the next. Every other value in the
	  file
	  is a whole number, a flag or a name, so nothing written by an
	  earlier
	  build changes meaning, and a whole ratio is still written whole.

