	  Hold the Windows client components to the /W4 warning bar
	  
	  The ACM codec and the DirectShow filter build again, but neither
	  had ever
	  been measured against the warning level the rest of the tree is
	  held to.
	  At /W4 they were not clean, and neither was the small sample
	  encoder that
	  sits in the same solution.
	  
	  Most of what the level reported is not this project's code: the
	  vendored
	  XML parser and the DirectShow base classes, which the filter
	  compiles
	  directly because it derives from the classes they implement.
	  Neither can
	  be acted on without forking it, so both are now compiled at their
	  own
	  author's warning level and the bar measures what is left.
	  
	  Of the rest, the unreferenced formal parameters are the case the
	  library
	  already suppresses for this compiler - debug-output stubs and
	  window
	  procedures that must accept a signature they do not use - so the
	  clients
	  now share that policy instead of being the only place it does not
	  apply.
	  The deliberate narrowing conversions are cast at the site, with
	  what
	  bounds the value written beside them, rather than disabled
	  wholesale.
	  
	  The remainder was ordinary: an assignment used as a condition,
	  three
	  locals that were never read, and two returns after a switch whose
	  every
	  case already returns.
	  
	  Two narrowing conversions in the ACM configuration writer are
	  left alone.
	  They put a fractional ratio into an integer attribute, so what
	  they change
	  is a stored value and not only its type; that is a question about
	  the
	  configuration format and is handled on its own.

