	  always built; the filter is built where its base class sources
	  are laid out,
	  and the generator says which cells it did not produce and why,
	  the same way it
	  already does for the optional libraries.
	  
	  Both cells end in a smoke test whose exit status is the cell's,
	  because a clean
	  build answers a different question than the one that matters for
	  these two.
	  They are DLLs that Windows loads into another program's process:
	  the test loads
	  each one, which runs its initialisation, resolves the entry
	  points it exists to
	  provide, and reads its import table for a runtime that would have
	  to be shipped
	  beside it. The import table is read from the binary rather than
	  taken from the
	  project settings, since that is the only place the answer
	  actually lives.
	  
	  The setup script lays the base class sources out like the other
	  dependencies.
	  Because they are a folder inside a sample repository rather than
	  a release
	  archive, it accepts either the repository archive or just that
	  folder, and
	  finds the right directory by a file only the base classes have
	  instead of
	  trusting the shape of the archive.

