	  Build the DirectShow filter against base class sources, not an
	  SDK
	  
	  Building the filter used to mean installing the Windows SDK 7.1,
	  opening a
	  solution from its samples folder, converting it and compiling two
	  of its
	  configurations first. That SDK has been retired for years, and
	  none of it was
	  ever necessary: the base classes compile against the current
	  Windows SDK with
	  the current toolset, and what the filter actually needs from them
	  is their
	  source, because the classes it derives from are implemented in
	  the .cpp files.
	  
	  The sources are now compiled into the filter and configured the
	  way every other
	  external dependency here is configured - one switch and one path
	  in a property
	  sheet - so getting them amounts to unpacking a folder. They are
	  treated as
	  external headers so that their warnings stay theirs, and the
	  filter is not
	  selected for building unless they are present, the same
	  arrangement the
	  analyzer has with its toolkit. Building it without them says
	  which switch to
	  set instead of failing on a missing include.

2026-08-16 10:27  aleidinger

	* dshow/Mpegac.cpp, vc_solution/vs_lame_dshow.vcxproj:
	  Link the DirectShow filter, and let its initialisation run
	  
	  More than half the unresolved symbols were the DirectShow class,
	  interface and
	  media type identifiers. They live in a library the project never
	  listed, so
	  every one of them was missing.
	  
	  The rest were the C runtime, and the cause was the project's own
	  entry point
	  override. Since Visual Studio 2015 the runtime is three
	  libraries, and the
	  directives naming two of them arrive with the startup object that
	  the linker
	  selects for the entry point it chose itself. Override that and
	  the other two
	  are never searched.
	  
	  Dropping the override alone would have been a silent regression
	  rather than a
	  fix: with no DllMain of its own the filter gets the runtime's
	  do-nothing stub,
	  links cleanly, and never initialises. It now provides one that
	  forwards, so the
	  runtime comes up first and the filter second.
	  
	  The filter is also a DLL that Windows loads into another
	  program's process, and
	  it now links the static runtime for the same reason as the codec
	  next to it.
	  
	  Finally the project and the linker disagreed about the name of
	  the file being
	  produced.

