	* libmp3lame/mpglib_interface.c:
	  Ask libmpg123 whether it answered before using the answer
	  
	  The encoder delay and padding are read into a local that is left
	  uninitialised and fetched by a call whose result was discarded.
	  Nothing
	  today makes that call fail, so nothing today goes wrong; but the
	  value
	  goes straight out to the caller as a count of samples to trim,
	  and there
	  is no version of that being worth leaving to whether a library
	  keeps
	  behaving as it does now. A call that does not answer now reports
	  the
	  figure as unavailable, which is what every caller already
	  handles.
	  
	  That is also the more useful reading of -1, and the documentation
	  gave
	  the other one: it said the figure did not fit. It usually means
	  the
	  stream never carried it, since these two live in the LAME tag and
	  most
	  of what a decoder meets has no such tag. A caller told only about
	  overflow would treat an ordinary file as a damaged one.

