	  file at a time: five of libmp3lame's sources had a page of their
	  own, and
	  the file index listed them, so a reader looking for the interface
	  was
	  offered the encoder's internals first. That was never a decision
	  - it is
	  what a documentation generator does by default when the
	  descriptions are
	  written next to the code they describe, and it survived the
	  earlier round
	  that stopped publishing the headers verbatim.
	  
	  The descriptions stay exactly where they are, at each function's
	  definition, and are now collected into a reference organised by
	  what a
	  caller is doing: encoding, settings, tags, decoding and version,
	  in the
	  order a program uses them. The public documentation carries no
	  file pages
	  at all, so nothing implementation-side appears in it; the
	  internal
	  documentation is unchanged and still shows every file. Every
	  entity that
	  was documented before is documented after, on the section page
	  for its
	  part of the interface.

2026-08-02 09:13  aleidinger

	* doc/html/history.html, doc/man/lame.1, frontend/parse.c,
	  libmp3lame/lame.c, test/unit/Makefile.am, test/unit/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/test_free_format.c:
	  Refuse a free format bitrate too low to carry a frame
	  
	  Free format takes the bitrate from the caller rather than from
	  the
	  standard's tables, so it can name a frame smaller than the side
	  information that frame has to carry - MPEG-1 at 48 kHz and 8
	  kbit/s is 24
	  bytes against 38. Nothing rejected it, and what happened next
	  depended on
	  the build: with assertions live the encoder ended the process
	  partway
	  through the first frame, and with them compiled out it reported
	  success
	  after writing megabytes of data that was not an MP3.
	  
	  It is now refused before encoding starts, and the report names
	  the lowest
	  bitrate that will work for the sample rate and channel count in
	  use. The
	  floor is the room the tightest stream the bitrate tables allow
	  leaves for
	  audio, so nothing that encodes today is affected. The manual page
	  said any
	  bitrate above 8 kbit/s could be used with this option, which is
	  true only
	  where a frame is large enough for one.

2026-08-02 09:12  aleidinger

	* doc/html/history.html, libmp3lame/util.h, test/unit/Makefile.am,
	  test/unit/Makefile.in, test/unit/test_bitstream_headers.c:
	  Keep the frame headers of a file that begins with a long silence
	  
	  At the format's low end - MPEG-2 at 8 kbit/s, two channels with
	  CRC - a
	  frame has one byte of room beyond its own side info, so a silent
	  lead-in
	  leaves one frame header waiting to be written for every eight
	  bits of the
	  bit reservoir. Past six seconds of silence there were more of
	  them than
	  the encoder had room for, and it overwrote the oldest rather than
	  keeping
	  it: the file lost the headers of the next 255 frames, while the
	  encoder
	  reported it once and returned success. There is now room for
	  twice as
	  many headers as the reservoir can call for, and no other setting
	  comes
	  near even the old limit.

2026-08-01 17:03  aleidinger

	* configure, configure.ac, test/unit/Makefile.am,
	  test/unit/Makefile.in, test/unit/test_rtp_socket.c:
	  Add unit tests for the RTP sender's destination handling
	  
	  The RTP sender had no test of any kind, and it is the one
	  frontend
	  component whose behaviour is a protocol rather than a file
	  format: what
	  it accepts as a destination, what it refuses, and what it puts on
	  the
	  wire.
	  
	  The tests read back what the sender connected to rather than
	  inferring
	  it, so they hold it to the address and the address family that
	  were
	  asked for, and one of them receives what the sender emits and
	  checks
	  the RTP header against the payload it was given. An IPv4-only
	  build is
	  covered as well: it has to refuse an IPv6 destination, not accept
	  it
	  and do something else with it.
	  
	  Loopback delivery is not something a unit test can insist on - a
	  host
	  firewall may drop it, and some do - so the test that needs it
	  probes
	  the path first and skips when nothing can get through. The
	  distinction
	  matters in the other direction too: whether a name resolves on
	  this
	  machine is asked separately from whether the sender can resolve
	  names,
	  because only the first is a reason to skip.
	  
	  The tests are built where the socket and resolver calls exist,
	  which is
	  the same answer that decides whether mp3rtp can be built at all.

2026-08-01 17:03  aleidinger

	* vc_solution/vs_gtk_config.props:
	  Stop holding GTK's headers to our own warning level
	  
	  The frame analyzer compiles GTK's headers as part of its own
	  translation units, so warnings from a dependency's headers were
	  reported against this project and moved with whatever GTK release
	  happened to be installed.
	  
	  Those include directories are now declared as external ones,
	  which the
	  compiler treats accordingly and which leaves our own sources at
	  the
	  level they had. That is preferable to naming individual warning
	  numbers in our source, which would have to be revisited whenever
	  the
	  dependency changes.

