	  vc_solution/Makefile.in, vc_solution/vs_lame_mp3rtp.vcxproj:
	  Make mp3rtp behave the same on every platform
	  
	  The RTP sender had two implementations, one for Windows and one
	  for
	  everything else, and they had drifted apart in what they do
	  rather than
	  only in how they spell it: only one of them resolved host names
	  or
	  accepted IPv6 destinations, only one enabled broadcast, and they
	  disagreed about the multicast loopback option and about how an
	  error is
	  reported. Which one a user got depended on their platform, and
	  the
	  manual page had to describe both.
	  
	  There is now one implementation. A destination may be an IPv4
	  address,
	  an IPv6 address or a host name everywhere, and the frontend no
	  longer
	  filters that argument through a character set no host name could
	  pass -
	  whether an address exists is the resolver's answer. A build for
	  IPv4
	  destinations only is still available, and is now offered on
	  Windows as
	  well, where there had been no such choice.
	  
	  Building it needed fixing too. Under MinGW mp3rtp could not be
	  built at
	  all: the configure check looked for the socket function in the C
	  library
	  and in libsocket, and on that platform it is in neither, so the
	  program
	  was refused. The check now links the calls the program really
	  makes, and
	  only mp3rtp links the library that provides them. The Visual
	  Studio
	  project and the nmake makefile name that library as well - the
	  interfaces replacing the deprecated ones are not in the one those
	  targets had been linking, and without the change the program does
	  not
	  link at all.
	  
	  Removed along the way: a block of #undef directives whose macros
	  are
	  defined nowhere this file can see. The preprocessed output is
	  unchanged
	  by their removal on both a Linux and a FreeBSD compiler, with and
	  without IPv6.
	  
	  This also clears the last deprecation warnings from the one
	  Windows
	  build configuration that compiles this file at the project's
	  warning
	  level.

