	  indeterminate
	  value after the jump, so that message could report a case other
	  than the
	  one that failed.
	  
	  It is also what kept the unit tests from being built with the
	  stricter
	  warnings turned on, which make the diagnostic fatal.

2026-08-01 08:33  aleidinger

	* libmp3lame/bitstream.c, libmp3lame/tables.c, libmp3lame/tables.h,
	  test/unit/Makefile.am, test/unit/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/test_huffman_tables.c:
	  Resolve the codeword-less Huffman table where it is used
	  
	  The table search can choose a table the standard defines no code
	  words
	  for, and it is the side information writer that turns that choice
	  into the
	  one the standard does define - as a side effect of writing the
	  header,
	  into the shared structure the code word writer reads afterwards.
	  So the
	  data half was safe only because the header half runs first, in
	  every path
	  there happens to be, and nothing said so.
	  
	  Both halves now ask for the codebook they are about to use, and
	  the search
	  result is left alone. The bitstream is unchanged: the same
	  substitution
	  happens, at the point where it is needed rather than before it.

2026-08-01 08:32  aleidinger

	* doc/html/history.html, frontend/lame_main.c, libmp3lame/lame.c,
	  libmp3lame/set_get.c, test/unit/Makefile.am,
	  test/unit/Makefile.in, test/unit/test_encode_clipgain.c:
	  Report no headroom figure for input that is entirely silent
	  
	  The clipping figures come from the loudest sample the encoder saw
	  in its
	  own decoded output. Material whose samples are all zero leaves
	  that at
	  zero, and the headroom is derived from its logarithm, so the
	  figure was
	  computed from an infinity and converted to an integer, which is
	  undefined.
	  What a user saw was a claim that the waveform is some two hundred
	  million
	  decibels away from full scale.
	  
	  Silence is ordinary input for this measurement - a muted track,
	  an empty
	  capture, a long leading gap - so it gets an answer rather than an
	  exception: no clipping, no scaling, and a message that says there
	  is no
	  headroom to report. The encoded audio is not affected; nor is any
	  input
	  that contains a signal.

