2026-07-30 19:05  aleidinger

	* Makefile.MSVC, doc/doxygen/vector-dispatch.dox,
	  libmp3lame/lame.c, libmp3lame/util.c, libmp3lame/util.h,
	  libmp3lame/vbrquantize.c, libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.am,
	  libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.in,
	  libmp3lame/vector/avx512_calc_sfb_noise.c,
	  libmp3lame/vector/lame_intrin.h, test/unit/Makefile.am,
	  test/unit/Makefile.in, test/unit/test_calc_sfb_noise_vector.c,
	  vc_solution/vs_libmp3lame.vcxproj,
	  vc_solution/vs_libmp3lame.vcxproj.filters:
	  Bring back the AVX-512 band-noise routine, behind a second switch
	  
	  The variable-bitrate noise estimate had an AVX-512 form. It was
	  written,
	  measured at 6.6% slower than the narrower code on a -V 2 encode,
	  and dropped on
	  that number. The number was honest, and it is now known to have
	  been taken in a
	  configuration nobody intended: the AVX-512 table-search routines
	  could not be
	  reached from an encode at all, so this routine was the only
	  512-bit code a -V
	  run executed, and it paid the frequency cost of entering the wide
	  registers by
	  itself. Alongside a table search that is already in them, the
	  same comparison
	  need not come out the same way.
	  
	  That is a question, not a claim, and it can only be answered on
	  hardware the
	  project does not have. So the routine returns compiled but
	  unreachable, under
	  LAME_AVX512_SFB_NOISE and the same alpha-only condition as the
	  switch beside
	  it, and the two together give a tester three arms: neither, the
	  table search
	  alone, and both. Only the last can show an interaction, and it is
	  the arm that
	  did not exist when the routine was rejected.
	  
	  Not un-rejected, then: nothing here changes what any build a user
	  can obtain
	  does, and the decision stands until there are numbers against it.
	  
	  The form selected is the bit-exact one - it quantizes four blocks
	  at a time but
	  folds their sums in the pinned association, so it computes what
	  the narrower
	  tier computes. Output identity is therefore something a tester
	  can check rather
	  than something they have to trade away, and a difference would be
	  a defect.

2026-07-30 19:04  aleidinger

	* ACM/ADbg/Makefile.in, ACM/Makefile.in, ACM/ddk/Makefile.in,
	  ACM/tinyxml/Makefile.in, Dll/Makefile.in, Makefile.in,
	  config.h.in, configure, configure.ac, doc/Makefile.in,
	  doc/doxygen/vector-dispatch.dox, doc/html/Makefile.in,
	  doc/html/history.html, doc/man/Makefile.in, dshow/Makefile.in,
	  frontend/Makefile.in, include/Makefile.in,
	  libmp3lame/Makefile.am, libmp3lame/Makefile.in,
	  libmp3lame/lame.c, libmp3lame/takehiro.c, libmp3lame/util.c,
	  libmp3lame/util.h, libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.am,
	  libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.in, libmp3lame/vector/lame_intrin.h,
	  libmp3lame/vector/neon_choose_table.c, mac/Makefile.in,
	  misc/Makefile.in, mpglib/Makefile.in, test/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/Makefile.am, test/unit/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/test_choose_table_neon.c, vc_solution/Makefile.in:
	  Add a NEON tier for the ARM vector routines
	  
	  ARM builds have run the plain C code: the vector routines are x86
	  intrinsics,
	  and until the selection was by name there was no way to offer a
	  set that is not
	  an x86 one. On the current shape this is a row in the table the
	  library already
	  keeps, so the option, the help text and the installed header gain
	  the set
	  without any of them being touched.
	  
	  One routine, not seven. Each of the x86 kernels was taken in turn
	  and measured
	  against what the compiler already produces on AArch64: three of
	  them clang
	  vectorises unprompted - including a native 32-bit signed maximum,
	  which is the
	  only reason the SSE2 sibling of that routine exists at all - and
	  two more were
	  written and came out slower than the scalar code. What pays is
	  the escape-coded
	  Huffman counting loop, worth about 3.5% of a 320 kbit/s encode
	  and about 1% of
	  a -V one. The internal ladder page records which is which, so the
	  four that are
	  absent read as a result rather than as work nobody got round to.
	  
	  The set is built wherever the compiler can build it and used
	  where the
	  processor reports it, on 64- and 32-bit ARM alike. Advanced SIMD
	  is
	  architectural on AArch64, so there the run-time question answers
	  itself; it is
	  optional on ARMv7, where the answer has to come from the
	  operating system, and
	  every one of them spells it differently.

2026-07-30 19:03  aleidinger

	* ACM/ADbg/Makefile.in, ACM/Makefile.in, ACM/ddk/Makefile.in,
	  ACM/tinyxml/Makefile.in, Dll/Makefile.in, Makefile.MSVC,
	  Makefile.in, config.h.in, configMS.h, configure, configure.ac,
	  doc/Makefile.in, doc/doxygen/internal.md,
	  doc/doxygen/vector-dispatch.dox, doc/html/Makefile.in,
	  doc/html/detailed.html, doc/html/history.html,
	  doc/man/Makefile.in, doc/man/lame.1, dshow/Makefile.in,
	  frontend/Makefile.in, include/Makefile.in,
	  libmp3lame/Makefile.am, libmp3lame/Makefile.in,
	  libmp3lame/lame.c, libmp3lame/takehiro.c, libmp3lame/util.c,
	  libmp3lame/util.h, libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.am,
	  libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.in,
	  libmp3lame/vector/avx512_choose_table.c,
	  libmp3lame/vector/avx512_quantize_lines.c,
	  libmp3lame/vector/lame_intrin.h, mac/Makefile.in,
	  misc/Makefile.in, mpglib/Makefile.in, test/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/Makefile.am, test/unit/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/test_choose_table_vector.c,
	  test/unit/test_quantize_lines_vector.c, vc_solution/Makefile.in,
	  vc_solution/vs_libmp3lame.vcxproj,
	  vc_solution/vs_libmp3lame.vcxproj.filters:
	  Add an AVX-512 tier for the constant-bitrate vector routines
	  
	  The vector ladder gains a rung above AVX2, carrying the
	  constant-bitrate
	  quantization loop. Three members of the lame-dev list measured it
	  on hardware
	  the project does not own: on an Intel Ice Lake part it is worth
	  about 6.5% of a
	  320 kbit/s encode, and on the two AMD processors it was measured
	  on it makes no
	  difference or costs about 1%.
	  
	  It is enabled wherever the processor can execute it, with no test
	  on vendor or
	  model. A percent at one setting on one microarchitecture does not
	  pay for a
	  dispatcher whose behaviour cannot be predicted from the hardware
	  alone, so the
	  trade-off is stated in the changelog instead of being encoded in
	  the selection.
	  The gate that confined the tier to alpha builds while its worth
	  was unknown is
	  gone with the question it existed for.
	  
	  The Huffman table search is a second question and is left open
	  rather than
	  answered by assumption. AVX-512 forms of it exist and are tested,
	  but the table
	  search is reached through a function pointer that only a per-tier
	  wrapper sets,
	  and no such wrapper was ever written - so those routines have
	  never executed
	  inside an encode, on any machine, and the measurements above
	  cannot be about
	  them. The wrapper is added here and installed only in an alpha
	  build with
	  LAME_AVX512_CHOOSE_TABLE set, which is a request for
	  measurements, not a
	  default. An alpha that has taken the path says so in the
	  --verbose header, so a
	  report can be told apart from a report about a run that did not.
	  
	  The variable-bitrate noise estimate deliberately has no AVX-512
	  version. One
	  was written, and it both failed to reproduce the narrower tier's
	  output exactly
	  - widening the sum breaks the association that keeps every
	  processor of an
	  architecture bit-identical - and ran 6.6% slower at -V 2 than the
	  code it would
	  have replaced, on the one machine where the tier is worth
	  anything at all. So
	  the top rung is a partial one: the internal ladder page records
	  what was built,
	  what each machine measured, and why the parts ended differently.
	  
	  Adding a set is a row in the table that answers every question
	  about vector
	  routines, so the installed header does not change.
	  
	  Measured and reported by Tobias Damisch, Elio Blanca and Todd
	  Zullinger.

2026-07-30 19:03  aleidinger

	* doc/html/detailed.html, doc/html/history.html, doc/man/lame.1,
	  frontend/parse.c, libmp3lame/lame.c:
	  Add --vector, and deprecate --noasm in favour of it
	  
	  --noasm can only take capability away: it says which
	  instruction-set families
	  the encoder may not use, and the answer to "run this encode on
	  SSE2 although
	  the processor also has AVX2" has to be assembled out of negations
	  - which stops
	  working the moment a machine has a set the option cannot name.
	  
	  --vector names the set to run instead. auto is the default and
	  keeps today's
	  behaviour, none is the plain C code, and a name pins the encode
	  to that set.
	  The names come from the library rather than from a list in the
	  frontend, so
	  --longhelp offers exactly what this binary can honour on this
	  processor, and a
	  name it cannot honour is refused rather than quietly replaced by
	  another - a
	  measurement that reports on a set nobody asked for is worse than
	  one that does
	  not run.
	  
	  Asking for none also leaves the processor unasked about its
	  capabilities,
	  nothing depending on the answer any more. For the users the
	  manual sends to
	  this option - the ones whose processor cannot survive the
	  question - that is
	  the whole of its value, so it is a documented property of none
	  rather than an
	  undocumented consequence of some flag being cleared elsewhere.
	  
	  --noasm keeps working and says what to use instead. Its mapping
	  is exact, so no
	  existing command line changes what it encodes: sse became none,
	  avx2 became
	  sse2, and mmx and 3dnow selected nothing to begin with. Where
	  both options
	  appear, --vector decides.

2026-07-30 19:02  aleidinger

	* doc/html/history.html, include/lame.h, libmp3lame/lame.c,
	  libmp3lame/lame_global_flags.h, libmp3lame/set_get.c,
	  libmp3lame/util.c, libmp3lame/util.h, test/unit/test_set_get.c:
	  Report the vector routines the encoder can actually use
	  
	  The configuration report listed processor features rather than
	  choices. It
	  named MMX, 3DNow! and SSE, none of which this library selects on
	  - the
	  vectorized routines are compiled for SSE2 - so the report could
	  not be
	  reconciled with what an encode did, and the line naming the set
	  actually used
	  was printed only when there was one, where its absence could not
	  be told apart
	  from a version that never printed it.
	  
	  The feature line now lists only the instruction sets this build
	  carries
	  routines for and this processor can run, which is exactly the
	  range a
	  selection may name, and the line reporting the outcome always
	  follows it. The
	  two together answer both halves of the question a benchmark run
	  asks: what
	  could have been used, and what was.
	  
	  MMX and 3DNow! had no code behind them and now have no state
	  either.
	  lame_set_asm_optimizations() refuses those two families and
	  lame_get_asm_optimizations() reports them as not enabled, rather
	  than storing
	  a flag whose only possible effect was to change a report; --noasm
	  mmx and
	  --noasm 3dnow are inert for the same reason. No encode changes.

2026-07-30 19:02  aleidinger

	* include/lame.h, include/libmp3lame.sym, libmp3lame/lame.c,
	  libmp3lame/lame_global_flags.h, libmp3lame/set_get.c,
	  libmp3lame/util.c, libmp3lame/util.h, test/unit/Makefile.am,
	  test/unit/Makefile.in, test/unit/test_vector_routines.c:
	  Add an API for selecting which vector routines the encoder runs
	  
	  The existing lame_set/get_asm_optimizations() pair names x86
	  instruction-set
	  families, and that has become the wrong shape for the question. A
	  family is
	  not what the library selects: the routines are compiled for SSE2,
	  while SSE
	  there is the group switch for all of them rather than a peer of
	  AVX2. Naming
	  each further instruction set, on this architecture or another,
	  would mean a
	  new value in a public enum in the installed header every time -
	  and none of
	  that lets a caller say "run this encode on SSE2 although the
	  processor also
	  has AVX2", which is what measuring one set against another needs.
	  
	  So the set of names becomes data rather than an enum. Four calls
	  report how
	  many sets this build compiled in and what they are called, select
	  one of them
	  by name, and report which one an instance ended up running.
	  Adding a set is a
	  table row; callers and the header are untouched.
	  
	  Names are the real instruction set, lowercase, and the displayed
	  spelling is
	  that name upper-cased, so there is one string per set and the two
	  cannot
	  drift. The same table answers the enumeration, the name lookup,
	  the selection
	  and the configuration report, because keeping those answers in
	  separate places
	  is how a build comes to advertise a set the encoder never
	  dispatches.
	  
	  A request that cannot be honoured is refused where it is made
	  rather than
	  quietly downgraded at initialisation: three distinct failures
	  separate a
	  misspelt name from a set this build lacks from one the processor
	  cannot
	  execute, since those ask for three different things from whoever
	  hit them.
	  
	  The default selects the widest set the processor offers, which is
	  what the
	  library did before, so an encode that asks for nothing is
	  unchanged.
	  
	  The older pair stays exported and keeps working; it is documented
	  as
	  deprecated. MMX and 3DNow! are called out further, because this
	  library has
	  had no code for either in years and they now select nothing at
	  all.

