	* Makefile.am, Makefile.in:
	  Make the bitstream test encode with the library it just built
	  
	  The test target encodes a reference file with the frontend from
	  this build and
	  counts how many bytes the result differs from the committed
	  reference. It runs
	  the frontend through libtool's wrapper, and on the systems where
	  that wrapper
	  is an executable rather than a shell script it does nothing to
	  the library
	  search path - so the frontend starts against whatever libmp3lame
	  the host has
	  installed.
	  
	  For this target that is not a cosmetic difference. The number it
	  prints exists
	  to tell a developer whether their change moved the encoder's
	  output; produced
	  by a different library, it describes that library instead, and a
	  build with no
	  change at all can report thousands of differing bytes. Where the
	  installed
	  library is old enough not to export everything the frontend
	  needs, the encode
	  does not run at all and the comparison is made against a file
	  that was never
	  written.
	  
	  Both rules that run a binary from this build now name the build's
	  own library
	  directory, which is what the unit tests were given for the same
	  reason. On the
	  systems where libtool already handled it nothing changes.

2026-07-28 18:40  aleidinger

	* test/unit/test_id3tag.c:
	  Decide a test's skip at compile time, not at run time
	  
	  One id3tag test declares a sample count large enough to drive the
	  play-length
	  field past a 32-bit millisecond value. That count does not fit an
	  unsigned
	  long everywhere, and the test knew it: it asked at run time and
	  skipped itself
	  where the type is narrower.
	  
	  Asking at run time is too late. The constant is still compiled,
	  so on a
	  platform with a 32-bit unsigned long it is truncated on the way
	  in and the
	  compiler says so - a warning on every build of the suite there,
	  about a value
	  the guard guarantees is never used. Moving the guard into the
	  preprocessor
	  keeps the behaviour identical on both kinds of platform and
	  leaves nothing for
	  the narrow one to complain about.
	  
	  No library code changes, and the test's result is unchanged
	  everywhere: it runs
	  where the value is representable and skips where it is not,
	  exactly as before.

2026-07-28 18:40  aleidinger

	* test/unit/Makefile.am, test/unit/Makefile.in:
	  Make the unit tests load the library they just built
	  
	  Every test in test/unit links libmp3lame, and on a machine that
	  already has
	  libmp3lame installed some of them were loading the installed copy
	  instead of
	  the one the build had just produced. A test suite that reports on
	  a library
	  nobody under test asked for is worse than one that fails: where
	  the installed
	  version happens to be close enough, the run is green and says
	  nothing.
	  
	  This was not visible on Unix, and the reason is worth stating
	  because it is
	  why nobody had noticed. Libtool's wrapper there is a shell script
	  and exports
	  a library search path pointing into the build tree, so the right
	  thing happens
	  by accident of the platform. On Windows libtool produces a
	  wrapper executable
	  instead, which does nothing equivalent, and the loader takes the
	  first match on
	  the ordinary search path - the installed one. Seven of the
	  fourteen programs
	  were running against a library several releases old; six failed
	  to start at
	  all, and one produced assertion failures that looked like defects
	  in this tree.
	  
	  Naming the build's own directory in the test environment makes
	  the answer the
	  same everywhere rather than a property of which wrapper libtool
	  chose. Both
	  search-path variables are set: the two families of systems
	  consult different
	  ones, and neither is harmful where it is not used.
	  
	  No library or test code changes.

2026-07-28 18:39  aleidinger

	* test/unit/test_id3tag.c:
	  Test the id3tag calls that select, reset and enumerate
	  
	  Second of the passes over the exported symbols no unit test
	  reached.
	  The existing id3tag tests cover the field setters across the
	  three text
	  encodings; what they never touch is the other half of the API -
	  the
	  calls that choose a tag version or a text encoding, reset the tag
	  state,
	  pad it, enumerate the genre list, or set the track number.
	  
	  The three deprecated UCS-2 setters are documented as aliases for
	  their
	  UTF-16 counterparts, so each is checked by building the same tag
	  twice,
	  once through the alias and once through its target, and comparing
	  the
	  two byte for byte. Looking for the frame instead would pass on an
	  alias
	  wired to the wrong function, which is the only way an alias can
	  be
	  wrong.
	  
	  Where a call has a documented consequence beyond its own field,
	  that
	  consequence is what is asserted: selecting UTF-8 is checked by
	  reading
	  the version out of the tag header, the space-padding option by
	  counting
	  the filler bytes of a known-width ID3v1 field against a build
	  without
	  it, and the padding shortcut by requiring it to produce the same
	  tag as
	  the general call with the size it documents.
	  
	  The genre enumeration is asserted to be a permutation - every
	  genre
	  reported exactly once - rather than merely the right length,
	  which a
	  list that repeated one entry and skipped another would also
	  satisfy.
	  
	  No library code changes.

