	* test/unit/Makefile.am, test/unit/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/test_lib_info.c, test/unit/test_set_get.c:
	  Test the version, MPEG-table and alias entry points
	  
	  Comparing the library's export list against the names the
	  unit-test
	  suite mentions leaves 63 of 242 exported symbols reached by no
	  test at
	  all. This covers the cheapest part of that gap: everything that
	  reports
	  a property of the build rather than of an encoder, and needs no
	  encoder
	  instance to call.
	  
	  The version tests are written against the API rather than against
	  the
	  version macros. Each string form is built at its own site, so
	  checking
	  the strings against the numerical form asks whether they agree -
	  which a
	  version bump that updates one site and not another can answer
	  wrongly,
	  where a test that rebuilt the macro expressions could not. Beyond
	  the
	  version number itself the strings are documented as unspecified,
	  and
	  nothing here asserts more than that; the one exception is the
	  compact
	  form, whose layout is fixed because callers copy it into
	  fixed-size
	  fields.
	  
	  The bitrate and sample-rate accessors exist so that a caller of
	  the
	  shared library can read tables that are not themselves exported.
	  Their
	  values come from the MPEG specification and cannot change, so
	  they are
	  asserted exactly, along with the range checking the accessors
	  add.
	  
	  Three entry points of the parameter API were missed by the
	  earlier pass
	  over it because they are not plain pairs: the announced input
	  length,
	  whose "length not known" default a caller has to be able to
	  recognize,
	  and the two deprecated aliases that combine or forward to the
	  decode-while-encoding settings. Whether those can be switched on
	  at all
	  depends on the build, so the tests ask the library instead of
	  assuming.
	  
	  No library code changes.

2026-07-28 18:38  aleidinger

	* doc/maintainer-abi.md, maintainer/abi/libmp3lame.abi:
	  Keep the build machine out of the committed ABI baseline
	  
	  The baseline ships in the release tarball so that a build from it
	  can run the
	  ABI check, but it recorded a good deal about the machine that
	  produced it: the
	  dynamic dependency list, declarations of libc functions the
	  library only calls,
	  and types that reached the dump through some other library's
	  headers. On a
	  glibc host built with the project's own settings that dependency
	  list names
	  libmvec, which does not exist on FreeBSD, so the shipped file
	  described one
	  operating system rather than the interface.
	  
	  It is now generated with the interface alone in it. Nothing about
	  what the
	  check can detect changes: a build missing an exported function is
	  still
	  reported as missing it, on either compiler tried.
	  
	  Two consequences worth having in the guide, both of which had
	  been learned the
	  hard way and written down nowhere. The compilation flags no
	  longer affect the
	  result, so the recipe no longer depends on a detail that quietly
	  changed
	  meaning when a user's CFLAGS began adding to the project's own
	  rather than
	  replacing them. And the file is architecture-specific while being
	  deliberately
	  compiler-independent, so the guide now says which of the three
	  checks to
	  disregard when running on an architecture other than the one the
	  baseline was
	  generated for.
	  
	  The guide also now states the rule that made all of this
	  confusing to work out:
	  a regeneration is read with abidiff, never with diff. The two
	  dumps of one
	  unchanged interface can differ by thousands of lines of XML and
	  mean nothing at
	  all.

