	  end-of-input rules that span all four implementation files, along
	  with
	  the test-only instrumentation and the regression routine that had
	  lived
	  in a plain text file beside them.

2026-07-27 19:27  aleidinger

	* configure, configure.ac:
	  build: locate iconv with the flags the frontend is built with
	  
	  The frontend's iconv check ran before any of the optional
	  libraries had been
	  located, so it tested the C library's <iconv.h> without the
	  include paths the
	  frontend is actually compiled with. Where those paths lead to a
	  GNU libiconv
	  installed beside the C library's iconv, its header renames
	  iconv_open to
	  libiconv_open - so the check answered for a different header than
	  the build
	  uses, reported that no separate library was needed, and the
	  frontend then
	  failed to link on an undefined libiconv_open.
	  
	  Whether it failed was decided by the installation prefix, because
	  the library
	  search only found that libiconv when the prefix being configured
	  happened to be
	  the one it was installed under. Configuring into any other prefix
	  - which is
	  what make distcheck does - could not link the frontend at all,
	  while the
	  default prefix concealed the problem entirely.
	  
	  The check now runs last, with the include and library search
	  paths pkg-config
	  reported for GTK, the decoder and libsndfile. They are taken from
	  what
	  pkg-config said rather than assumed, since an installation prefix
	  is
	  configurable and not always the conventional one. Only the -I and
	  -L options
	  are carried over: the link options are written for libtool, which
	  links the
	  frontend, and include flags that a bare compiler rejects
	  outright. Where no
	  such paths exist nothing is added and the check behaves exactly
	  as before -
	  nothing can bring in a foreign <iconv.h> without also saying
	  where its library
	  lives.
	  
	  The configuration summary now reports iconv as well. Tag
	  transliteration is
	  compiled out when iconv is unavailable, so an unnoticed negative
	  result would
	  silently drop a feature instead of failing the build.

2026-07-27 19:26  aleidinger

	* maintainer/check-dist.sh:
	  maintainer: fix the tarball-name check on a non-GNU sed
	  
	  The distribution check compares the version in the tarball's name
	  against the
	  one configure declares and the one version.h carries. It stripped
	  the archive
	  suffix with a basic regular expression using \| alternation,
	  which is a GNU
	  extension: on a BSD sed the suffix was never stripped, the
	  comparison could
	  not match, and the check reported the tarball as misnamed. It has
	  done that on
	  every FreeBSD run it has ever made, while passing on Linux, which
	  is why the
	  mistake survived - the platform where it works is the one it was
	  written on.
	  
	  One plain expression per suffix does the same job in a basic
	  regular
	  expression on either sed.

2026-07-27 19:26  aleidinger

	* maintainer/gen-build-matrix.ps1:
	  build: stop handing the 32-bit decoder to a 64-bit matrix cell
	  
	  The Windows matrix generator looks the optional decoder up once,
	  for the nmake
	  cells, which are 32-bit. It then passed that same path to the
	  MSBuild decoder
	  cell, which is built for the selected platform - by default x64.
	  On a machine
	  holding only the 32-bit library that cell could not resolve a
	  single decoder
	  symbol, and the matrix reported a failure that was its own doing
	  rather than
	  the tree's.
	  
	  The project file already resolves the decoder per platform, so
	  the cell only
	  has to turn the option on and leave the path alone, exactly as
	  the libsndfile
	  cell beside it does. It is now emitted only when the library for
	  that platform
	  is actually present, so a missing one drops the cell instead of
	  failing it.

