	* ACM/ADbg/Makefile.in, ACM/Makefile.in, ACM/ddk/Makefile.in,
	  ACM/tinyxml/Makefile.in, Dll/Makefile.in, Makefile.in, configure,
	  configure.ac, doc/Makefile.in, doc/html/Makefile.in,
	  doc/man/Makefile.in, dshow/Makefile.in, frontend/Makefile.am,
	  frontend/Makefile.in, frontend/README.mp3x-gtk4,
	  frontend/mp3x_core.c, frontend/mp3x_core.h, frontend/mp3x_plot.c,
	  frontend/mp3x_plot.h, frontend/mp3x_session.c,
	  frontend/mp3x_session.h, frontend/mp3x_ui.c, include/Makefile.in,
	  libmp3lame/Makefile.in, libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.in,
	  mac/Makefile.in, misc/Makefile.in, mpglib/Makefile.in,
	  test/Makefile.in, test/unit/Makefile.in, vc_solution/Makefile.in:
	  frontend: replace the mp3x frame analyzer with a GTK4
	  implementation
	  
	  The frame analyzer was written against GTK 1.2, which has not
	  been
	  available on current systems for many years, so in practice mp3x
	  could
	  no longer be built. This is a reimplementation on GTK 4, keeping
	  the
	  program name, the command line, the analyzer data and all eight
	  plots,
	  and adding what a desktop program is expected to have: opening
	  and
	  replacing a file from within the program, a recent-files list,
	  and PNG
	  export of the graphs.
	  
	  Frame processing, session lifetime, plotting and the user
	  interface are
	  separated so that replacing the open file and shutting down have
	  clear
	  ownership boundaries. The analyzer engine itself stays toolkit
	  independent.
	  
	  mp3x now requires GTK 4.10 and a decoder-enabled build, and GTK
	  is
	  probed with pkg-config rather than the obsolete gtk-config
	  script. At
	  end of input the delayed encoder and decoder output is drained,
	  so the
	  final frames are analyzed rather than dropped.
	  
	  The GTK 1.2 sources are no longer compiled but stay in the
	  release
	  archive.
	  
	  Patch by Maya R. Odinezenko, SF patch #104.

2026-07-27 19:23  aleidinger

	* ACM/ADbg/Makefile.in, ACM/Makefile.in, ACM/ddk/Makefile.in,
	  ACM/tinyxml/Makefile.in, Dll/Makefile.in, Makefile.in, configure,
	  configure.ac, doc/Makefile.in, doc/html/Makefile.in,
	  doc/man/Makefile.in, dshow/Makefile.in, frontend/Makefile.am,
	  frontend/Makefile.in, frontend/main.c, include/Makefile.in,
	  libmp3lame/Makefile.in, libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.in,
	  mac/Makefile.in, misc/Makefile.in, mpglib/Makefile.in,
	  test/Makefile.in, test/unit/Makefile.in, vc_solution/Makefile.in:
	  frontend: handle Unicode command lines and paths on MinGW
	  
	  MinGW builds of the command line tools lose characters that are
	  not in
	  the active code page. The narrow entry point receives an already
	  lossy command line, and filenames are handed to the narrow
	  fopen(),
	  so a path the user can see in Explorer may be unopenable.
	  
	  Use the Windows wide-character entry point for the MinGW frontend
	  programs, convert the UTF-16 argument array to UTF-8 before the
	  common
	  frontend sees it, and open files through the existing
	  wide-character
	  helpers. -municode is applied only when linking the frontend
	  programs;
	  a library-only build needs no Windows startup object, and a MinGW
	  frontend build whose toolchain cannot provide that startup is
	  refused
	  rather than silently built without it.
	  
	  The UTF-8 to UTF-16 conversion is also made strict: an invalid
	  sequence
	  is now an error instead of being silently replaced. This affects
	  the
	  Microsoft toolchain too, which already used the wide entry point.
	  
	  The wide entry point and the strict conversion are one change and
	  not
	  two: with the strict conversion in place but the narrow entry
	  point
	  still selected, MinGW would reject the very command line it is
	  given.
	  
	  MSVC builds and every non-Windows platform are unaffected - the
	  added
	  code is inside the existing _WIN32 branch and the added configure
	  logic inside a MinGW host case.
	  
	  Patch by Maya R. Odinezenko, SF patch #104.

