	* doc/html/history.html, libmp3lame/mpglib_interface.c:
	  fix: refuse to create a decoder when the library cannot decode
	  
	  A libmp3lame built without libmpg123 cannot decode anything, and
	  only one of
	  the two calls that create a decoder said so. The other returned a
	  decoder
	  that failed on every use, so the absence surfaced at the first
	  piece of audio
	  rather than at the point it could be acted on - and there it
	  looks like a
	  problem with the input, not with the build. Both calls now return
	  nothing,
	  which a caller can check before reading any data.
	  
	  The failure a caller sees is unchanged in kind, only in timing: a
	  program that
	  already checks what it gets back needs no change, and one that
	  does not fails
	  at the same call it failed at before. Nothing changes in a build
	  that has the
	  decoder, which is every default build.
	  
	  The path that reported the absence was also leaking the instance
	  it had just
	  allocated; it is freed now, in both calls.

2026-07-27 11:36  aleidinger

	* doc/doxygen/internal.md, doc/doxygen/vector-dispatch.dox:
	  Record why the vector dispatch ladder stops at AVX2
	  
	  The internal dispatch page explains the two rungs that are
	  missing for
	  counter-intuitive reasons. A third belongs beside them now: an
	  AVX-512
	  tier above AVX2 was written, taken to hardware that has the
	  instruction
	  set, and measured end to end - and it is not worth carrying.
	  
	  Three machines reported. On Zen 4, constant bitrate at 320 kbit/s
	  - the
	  case with the most for such a tier to win - came out 1.0% slower
	  than
	  the AVX2 tier, with the runs each way not overlapping, and the
	  same
	  result came back from a second run on a different input file. On
	  Zen 5
	  the same comparison returned equal figures to three decimals over
	  twenty-five runs each way. Variable bitrate was a wash
	  everywhere. An
	  Ice Lake laptop reported a 7.6% gain, but from three sequential
	  runs per
	  arm on a thermally constrained part, and its own data shows
	  roughly a
	  fifth of that as drift between two runs of identical code; the
	  page
	  records it as the one measurement still outstanding rather than
	  as a
	  result.
	  
	  The kernels produced bit-identical output, so this is a statement
	  about
	  the width and not about the code. It is also not the statement
	  everyone
	  expected about double-pumped datapaths: Zen 5 does not carry that
	  restriction and still measured zero. What binds is the work
	  itself -
	  loops that are already gather-bound, or bound by a fixed
	  reduction
	  order, stay that way when the registers get wider. Widening the
	  one
	  routine whose reduction order could have been changed cost it
	  exact
	  agreement with the existing tier on both machines that ran the
	  tests,
	  which is a correctness answer rather than a speed one.
	  
	  This goes in the ladder page rather than only in the commit
	  history
	  because a negative result nobody wrote down is a negative result
	  someone
	  re-derives. The section records what was tested and on which
	  hardware,
	  not just the conclusion, and states what would reopen the
	  question.
	  
	  Measured and reported by Tobias Damisch, Elio Blanca and Todd
	  Zullinger.

