	* frontend/get_audio.c, libmp3lame/bitstream.c,
	  libmp3lame/gain_analysis.c, libmp3lame/mpglib_interface.c,
	  libmp3lame/set_get.c:
	  build: make the non-default build configurations warning-free too
	  
	  The tree's warning-free state was established in one build
	  configuration.
	  Measured across the configuration matrix it did not hold in three
	  others:
	  a build without the decoder, a build without the analyzer hooks,
	  and -
	  in every cell, including the default one - any GCC-family
	  compiler on
	  Windows. None of this is visible from an ordinary Linux build,
	  which is
	  why it survived the earlier cleanup.
	  
	  Three causes, none of them a behaviour change:
	  
	  Parameters that exist only for the decoder or the analyzer are
	  unused
	  exactly when those are configured out. They now carry the
	  project's
	  "may be unused" attribute, which is what the earlier sweep
	  applied to
	  the parameters that were unused in the default build.
	  
	  An MSVC-specific pragma was guarded by a macro that MinGW defines
	  as
	  well, so every GCC or Clang build on Windows parsed a pragma it
	  does not
	  know and said so, twice. It now keys off the MSVC compiler macro,
	  the
	  guard the rest of the tree already uses for the same purpose.
	  
	  The audio reader asked the same question about the input format
	  three
	  times over. One local answers it once, which reads better and
	  also
	  settles a possibly-uninitialized diagnostic that the repetition
	  provoked: the compiler could not know the three answers agree,
	  and in a
	  build without the decoder it saw a buffer that is filled under
	  one of
	  them and read under another.

