	* test/unit/parse_test_stubs.c:
	  test: stub the frontend helpers the option parser reaches
	  elsewhere
	  
	  Three unit tests compile a frontend source file directly, because
	  the
	  functions they exercise are not visible outside it, and they link
	  that
	  one translation unit against a small set of stand-ins instead of
	  the
	  whole frontend. Several of the functions it calls sit behind
	  conditions
	  the usual build does not meet - a build configured without the
	  decoder,
	  a debug or unoptimized build, Windows - so no stand-in was ever
	  written
	  for them and "make check" fails to link in each of those
	  configurations.
	  
	  In the usual build the suite links only because the compiler
	  discards
	  the one remaining call together with the branch it sits in.
	  Lowering
	  the optimization level is enough to break the test suite, which
	  makes
	  the green result an accident of how the tree happens to be built
	  rather
	  than a property of the tests.
	  
	  The missing stand-ins are supplied, so the suite builds wherever
	  the
	  project does. They are defined unconditionally, and the real
	  prototypes
	  are included rather than repeated: a signature that drifts then
	  fails to
	  compile instead of failing to link, and the test-side file does
	  not have
	  to track conditions that live in the frontend.

2026-07-27 11:35  aleidinger

	* doc/html/history.html, libmp3lame/quantize_pvt.c:
	  fix: make the two ATH-only masking options reach the quantizer
	  again
	  
	  The absolute threshold of hearing is the quiet-room curve, the
	  level below
	  which a tone is inaudible with nothing else playing. In an
	  ordinary encode
	  it is only the floor, and the masking the psychoacoustic model
	  derives from
	  the signal itself does the real work. Two options ask for the
	  floor alone
	  instead - one for every block, one for short blocks only - which
	  is a way
	  to hear what the model contributes, and to compare an encode made
	  without
	  it.
	  
	  Neither had any effect on the encoded audio. The place where the
	  model's
	  threshold is folded in over the floor no longer consulted them,
	  so the two
	  settings survived only in the analyzer's display and in the flag
	  that marks
	  an encode as non-standard. A caller could set either, hear no
	  difference,
	  and have nothing to point at. The fold is now skipped when the
	  corresponding option is set, which is what the analyzer's own
	  handling of
	  the same two settings had gone on doing all along.
	  
	  Both options are off by default and the guards are unreachable
	  without
	  them, so an ordinary encode is untouched: the output is byte for
	  byte
	  identical for CBR and for VBR. With either option set the output
	  changes,
	  which is the point, and was confirmed rather than assumed - a run
	  with each
	  option differs both from the same build without it and from the
	  previous
	  behaviour.

2026-07-27 11:35  aleidinger

	* doc/html/history.html, libmp3lame/set_get.c,
	  test/unit/test_set_get.c:
	  fix: let the temporal masking getter report that nothing has been
	  chosen
	  
	  Several encoder settings are stored with a negative marker
	  meaning "not
	  chosen yet", which the initialization resolves to a real default.
	  Reading
	  one of them back before then is a legal thing for a caller to do,
	  and it
	  is how a caller learns that a setting is still at whatever the
	  encoder
	  will pick.
	  
	  The getter for the temporal masking setting asserted its value
	  was zero or
	  one, which the marker is not, so that call aborted the process
	  instead of
	  answering. Assertions are deliberately left enabled in release
	  builds of
	  this library, so this was not confined to a debugging
	  configuration. The
	  neighbouring setting with the same shape already admits its own
	  marker;
	  this one now does the same.
	  
	  The unit tests gained a case for the whole class: every setting
	  that has
	  such a marker is read on a freshly created encoder, and read
	  again after
	  initialization to confirm the marker does not survive it. The
	  existing
	  tests could not have caught this, because they all set a value
	  before
	  reading it back and so never see the marker. The new case fails
	  against
	  the unfixed source, which is what makes it worth having.
	  
	  Behaviour is otherwise unchanged: the getter returns what it
	  always
	  returned once a value has been chosen, and no encoding path reads
	  it
	  through this function. The encoded bitstream is identical for CBR
	  and
	  VBR.

