	* libmp3lame/id3tag.c:
	  doc: document the metadata API for the public API reference
	  
	  Completes the public API documentation with the ID3 tagging
	  interface -
	  the last of its files the reference did not cover. That file is
	  described too, so the tagging API has a page of its own.
	  
	  The properties that apply to all of it are stated once: the tags
	  have to
	  be set before the encoder is initialized, since the ID3v2 tag
	  precedes the
	  audio; which tags get written is a choice of its own; and the
	  setters that
	  return nothing cannot report a failure, including a failure to
	  allocate.
	  
	  The recurring hazard in this interface is silent truncation, so
	  each field
	  says what it costs. ID3v1 fields are fixed-length, and text
	  longer than
	  the field is cut without a word - which means the same call can
	  be exact
	  in one tag and lossy in the other. The comment field is the
	  sharpest
	  case: it loses two characters as soon as a track number is set,
	  because
	  those bytes are what carries the track number, so a comment that
	  was
	  accepted can be shortened by a later call about something else.
	  
	  Where a function reports something, the reference says what the
	  report
	  actually means. Setting a track number returns an error for a
	  value ID3v1
	  cannot hold, although the value is written to ID3v2 and the call
	  did what
	  it could. Setting a genre accepts a name outside the standard
	  list
	  without complaint, and replaces a name that is in the list with
	  the list's
	  own spelling. Attaching cover art determines the image format
	  from the
	  data rather than trusting the caller, and refuses anything it
	  does not
	  recognize. The two functions that hand a rendered tag back are
	  documented
	  with the buffer-sizing convention they share, in which the result
	  has to
	  be compared against the size passed in to know whether anything
	  was
	  copied.
	  
	  Documentation only: the comment-stripped source is unchanged byte
	  for
	  byte, so neither the library's behaviour nor its interface is
	  affected.

