	* libmp3lame/mpglib_interface.c:
	  doc: document the decoding API for the public API reference
	  
	  The library ships a decoder alongside the encoder, because
	  encoding needs
	  one: measuring what a file will sound like means decoding it
	  again. It is
	  exposed for its own sake as a small API of its own, and until now
	  the
	  public reference did not mention it at all. The file it lives in
	  is
	  described too, so the decoder has a page of its own.
	  
	  The entry points are documented with the two properties a caller
	  has to
	  know before anything else. The decoder is a separate object from
	  the
	  encoder, created and destroyed by its own calls. And the decoding
	  itself
	  is done by an optional external library: a build without it
	  cannot decode,
	  and neither of the two calls that create a decoder hands one
	  back, so
	  checking that result is what tells a caller whether decoding is
	  available
	  before any input has been read.
	  
	  Each decode entry point states how much output space it requires,
	  which is
	  where the two families differ and where a caller can go wrong
	  quietly: the
	  one-frame calls need room for a frame, while the convenience call
	  that
	  consumes a whole buffer needs room for every frame in it and
	  cannot check.
	  A return of zero is documented as the normal state at the start
	  of a
	  stream rather than as a failure.
	  
	  The three functions that would route the decoder's diagnostics
	  accept a
	  callback and discard it, so nothing is reported however they are
	  called;
	  they say so. The obsolete entry points from the era when the
	  decoder was
	  a single global object are marked obsolete and record that they
	  return an
	  error without decoding, which matters because a caller that
	  ignores the
	  result reads output buffers that were never written.
	  
	  Documentation only: the comment-stripped source is unchanged byte
	  for
	  byte, so neither the library's behaviour nor its interface is
	  affected.

