	* libmp3lame/set_get.c:
	  doc: document the psychoacoustic settings for the public API
	  reference
	  
	  Continues the public API documentation with the section the
	  source itself
	  introduces as the one not to touch without knowing what you are
	  doing:
	  the masking offsets, the absolute threshold of hearing and its
	  adaptive
	  adjustment, the block-type coupling, temporal and inter-channel
	  masking,
	  and substep noise shaping.
	  
	  Two of these settings no longer affect the encoded audio. Asking
	  for
	  masking against the threshold of hearing alone, whether for all
	  blocks or
	  only for short ones, is not read by the quantizer any more; what
	  remains
	  is that one of the two still marks the encode as non-standard in
	  the VBR
	  header, which is a durable property of the file even though the
	  audio is
	  identical. Saying so is the point of documenting them - the
	  alternative
	  is a caller changing a setting, hearing no difference, and
	  concluding
	  something else is wrong. Dropping the threshold entirely, which
	  looks
	  like the same kind of switch, does still work, and the reference
	  now
	  distinguishes the three.
	  
	  Several settings resolve their default during initialization
	  rather than
	  at instance creation, so the same getter answers differently
	  before and
	  after it with nobody having chosen anything. Each such entry says
	  so and
	  sends the caller to the getter, rather than naming the value:
	  what an
	  unset setting resolves to is the encoder's business and not
	  something
	  this interface undertakes to keep. One of them cannot be read
	  back even
	  after initialization; documenting it made clear that this is a
	  gap rather
	  than a decision, so the entry says so and carries a to-do to
	  close it.
	  Two others can never be returned to the unresolved state, because
	  the
	  setter accepts a narrower range of values than the field holds.
	  
	  Where a setting is a packed word rather than the number or flag
	  its name
	  suggests, the layout is written out: which bits enable substep
	  shaping,
	  extend it to short blocks, or select its variant. The block-type
	  coupling
	  entry records that it shares one field with two other functions,
	  so the
	  last one called wins, and that a stereo encode overrides the
	  request
	  during initialization regardless.
	  
	  Documentation only: the comment-stripped source is unchanged byte
	  for
	  byte, so neither the library's behaviour nor its interface is
	  affected.

