	* libmp3lame/set_get.c:
	  doc: document the bitrate modes and filters for the public API
	  reference
	  
	  Continues the public API documentation with the choice between
	  constant,
	  average and variable bitrate, the quality level and the bitrate
	  bounds
	  that go with it, and the lowpass and highpass filters.
	  
	  The quality level is documented as running the opposite way to a
	  bitrate,
	  0 being the best, and both entry points that set it are recorded
	  as
	  clamping an out-of-range value and returning an error for it
	  anyway - a
	  combination a caller will otherwise read as "nothing happened".
	  
	  Several of these settings are rewritten during initialization,
	  and the
	  reference now says so, because the alternative is a caller
	  concluding the
	  library ignored them. The bitrate bounds are snapped to the rates
	  the
	  format actually offers, so asking for one number and reading back
	  a
	  nearby one is correct behaviour. The target average is clamped
	  into the
	  window the bounds leave, and it is not confined to the average
	  bitrate
	  mode: with a constant bitrate and no bitrate set, it becomes the
	  bitrate.
	  
	  The two filters look symmetrical and are not, which is the single
	  most
	  useful thing to know about them. A lowpass cutoff of zero asks
	  LAME to
	  pick one, and picking one can change the output sample rate,
	  because the
	  encoder then chooses the lowest rate that still carries the
	  cutoff. A
	  highpass cutoff of zero means no highpass at all: nothing derives
	  one,
	  so the filter exists only if a caller asks for it. The transition
	  widths
	  are preferences in both cases, since the polyphase filter can
	  only place
	  a transition on a band boundary.
	  
	  Documentation only: the comment-stripped source is unchanged byte
	  for
	  byte, so neither the library's behaviour nor its interface is
	  affected.

