	* libmp3lame/set_get.c:
	  doc: document the frame and quantization parameters for the
	  public API reference
	  
	  Continues the public API documentation with the settings that
	  shape the
	  frames themselves - the four header flag bits, the optional
	  per-frame
	  checksum and the bit reservoir - and with the quantization and
	  noise
	  shaping controls that follow them.
	  
	  The header flags are documented as what they are: declarations
	  LAME
	  writes through and never acts on, one of which defaults to set
	  rather
	  than clear. The checksum entry states its price, two bytes per
	  frame
	  taken out of the space left for audio, and warns that the header
	  bit
	  carries the opposite sense to the setting.
	  
	  Three of these are not the booleans their names suggest. The ISO
	  compliance setting is a three-way choice of how large a bit
	  reservoir the
	  bitstream may rely on, and its default is the most permissive of
	  the
	  three rather than the zero the name implies. The psychoacoustic
	  tuning
	  entry point is a single integer packing six unrelated fields,
	  whose
	  layout and quarter-decibel encoding a caller has no way to
	  discover; it
	  is now written out, together with the advice to compose the value
	  against
	  what is already there instead of assigning it. The two
	  quantization
	  comparison settings are unset by default and acquire different
	  values
	  during initialization, so reading one before and after
	  initializing gives
	  two different answers with nobody having set anything.
	  
	  The remaining experimental switches are described by what they do
	  to the
	  encode - one gives up the extra bits normally spent on the
	  topmost
	  scalefactor band, the other forces a short-block analysis the
	  model would
	  otherwise skip - and the obsolete padding control is marked
	  obsolete and
	  states that it reports success unconditionally, having nothing
	  left to
	  fail at.
	  
	  Documentation only: the comment-stripped source is unchanged byte
	  for
	  byte, so neither the library's behaviour nor its interface is
	  affected.

