	* Makefile.am, Makefile.in, doc/Doxyfile-internal.in,
	  doc/Makefile.am, doc/Makefile.in, doc/doxygen/internal.md,
	  doc/maintainer-gen-api-docs.md, maintainer/gen-api-docs.sh:
	  Add a "gen-api-docs" script to publish the API documentation
	  
	  The generated documentation has so far only ever existed in
	  whoever's
	  build directory produced it. This adds the step that puts it
	  where
	  people can read it: build both sets - the public library
	  reference and
	  the internal developer documentation - and copy them into the
	  website
	  working tree.
	  
	  It stops at the working tree. Committing and uploading the
	  website is
	  the maintainer's step, the same as committing to the repository,
	  so the
	  script writes, says exactly what it wrote, and gets out of the
	  way.
	  
	  Both sets are published, which is a decision rather than a
	  default: the
	  internal one documents structures and functions that carry no
	  stability
	  promise at all. It is therefore linked only from the
	  developer-facing
	  pages, and never without saying what it is not.
	  
	  The copy is a clean copy, so a page that no longer exists stops
	  being
	  served rather than lingering. And nothing is copied at all unless
	  the
	  generated documentation contains something that could only have
	  come
	  from parsed source: a run that reads no input still exits
	  successfully
	  and still writes a complete set of stylesheets and images, and
	  copying
	  that over the website would quietly replace a working reference
	  with an
	  empty one.
	  
	  The generated HTML is not added to the distribution; it is
	  reproducible
	  from the tarball, and a second copy would only go stale.

