	* Makefile.am, Makefile.in, doc/Doxyfile-internal.in,
	  doc/Makefile.am, doc/Makefile.in, doc/doxygen/internal.md,
	  doc/maintainer-build-matrix.md, doc/maintainer-check-dist.md,
	  maintainer/check-dist.ps1, maintainer/check-dist.sh,
	  maintainer/gen-build-matrix.sh:
	  Add a "check-dist" validator for a finished distribution tarball
	  
	  Rolling a release currently ends with a tarball nobody has tried.
	  This
	  adds a script that takes one, unpacks it, builds it in every
	  configuration the machine can build, runs the tests in each, and
	  reports
	  every step as a named check with a short description of what it
	  establishes and a verdict.
	  
	  Everything it uses comes out of the tarball rather than the tree
	  it was
	  rolled from - the build harness, the ABI check, the man pages -
	  because
	  a file missing from the tarball is exactly the defect being
	  looked for.
	  It continues through all its checks and only exits non-zero at
	  the end,
	  since a release candidate is more useful fully surveyed than
	  abandoned
	  at the first problem.
	  
	  A check that cannot be made reports one of two verdicts rather
	  than one.
	  SKIP means a prerequisite is missing on this machine and
	  installing it
	  would produce a real answer; not applicable means the check has
	  no
	  meaning on this path at all and nothing installable would change
	  that.
	  The distinction is what makes a log from somebody else's machine
	  readable, and it is what lets the PowerShell companion for the
	  native
	  Windows build be honest about the checks that only exist in the
	  autotools build.
	  
	  The build harness gained one option along the way: extra
	  configure
	  arguments to be added to every cell. The per-cell unit tests need
	  it -
	  without it no cell enables them, so those checks would have
	  reported
	  "skipped" for ever while looking like they were being run.

