	* frontend/get_audio.c, test/unit/test_get_audio_aiff.c:
	  get_audio: range-check the AIFF sample rate before converting it
	  
	  The sample rate declared in an AIFF header reaches the encoder as
	  an int,
	  with nothing checking that the conversion is defined. It is not,
	  for any
	  value outside that type's range, and a crafted COMM chunk can
	  declare
	  three kinds of such a value: an infinity, which is what an
	  all-ones
	  exponent means; a finite rate far above the range; and a negative
	  one. In
	  practice each converts to something the encoder then refuses, so
	  the file
	  is rejected either way - but only by accident of what the
	  conversion
	  happens to produce.
	  
	  Validate the rate where the chunk's other fields are validated
	  and treat
	  one that cannot be represented as the malformed field it is.
	  Nothing a
	  real encoder writes is affected. The unit test covers each of the
	  three,
	  none of which it reached before.
	  
	  Frontend-only; the library and the bitstream are untouched.

