	* configure, configure.ac, libmp3lame/lame.c,
	  test/unit/test_encode_nonfinite.c:
	  Keep the non-finite PCM check out of the optimizer's reach
	  
	  The floating point encoding entry points screen their input for
	  NaN and
	  infinity, because such a sample spreads through the psycho
	  acoustic model and
	  trips an assertion inside it. The library is built with
	  -ffast-math, which
	  tells the compiler that no such value ever occurs and so entitles
	  it to delete
	  the screen. Compilers have begun acting on that: from clang 21
	  onwards the
	  screen is absent from the built library on every platform, and
	  one non-finite
	  sample from the caller terminates the application. gcc and
	  earlier clang keep
	  it, which is why this presented as a single platform misbehaving.
	  
	  Spelling the test in terms of the raw exponent bits does not
	  avoid it - the
	  integer comparison is recognised as a floating point class test
	  and folded just
	  as isfinite() would be. The value instead reaches the comparison
	  through a
	  volatile object, which the compiler may not reason across. The
	  cost does not
	  rise above the run-to-run spread of an encode.
	  
	  The unit test needs the same treatment for the same reason: built
	  with the
	  library's flags it could not construct a non-finite sample to
	  test with, so it
	  failed whether or not the library was sound - and would have gone
	  on failing
	  after a fix.
	  
	  The comment on the optimization flags gains the general point,
	  since the flag
	  is where this is worth knowing about.

2026-07-27 11:23  aleidinger

	* test/unit/test_set_get.c:
	  test: keep the parameter-API test comment self-contained

2026-07-27 11:23  aleidinger

	* configure, configure.ac, doc/maintainer-abi.md:
	  Let a user's CFLAGS add to the build's flags instead of replacing
	  them
	  
	  Passing CFLAGS to configure suppressed the project's own
	  optimization
	  settings entirely. One flag on the command line was enough to
	  drop
	  -ffast-math, which the DSP code is validated with and which
	  changes the
	  encoder's output, along with the rest of them - silently, and
	  with
	  nothing in the build log to say so.
	  
	  They are now always applied, with the given CFLAGS placed after
	  them, so
	  it adds to the build and overrides only what it names.
	  CFLAGS='-g' asks
	  for debug information and keeps the optimization; CFLAGS='-O2 -g'
	  asks
	  for both and still keeps the arithmetic settings. Anything can
	  still be
	  countermanded by naming its opposite, since the later flag wins.
	  
	  A default build is unchanged, flag for flag.
	  
	  The maintainer guide for the ABI check asked for CFLAGS='-g -O2'
	  to get
	  the debug information its deepest check needs, which was this
	  problem in
	  miniature; it now asks for CFLAGS='-g'.

2026-07-27 11:22  aleidinger

	* frontend/get_audio.c, include/lame.h, libmp3lame/util.h:
	  Declare the decoder analysis hooks in the public header
	  
	  Both are exported from the library and called across the library
	  boundary by the frontends, but they were declared only in an
	  internal
	  header. An analyzer frontend outside the tree therefore had to
	  write the
	  prototypes itself, which is what our own frontend did too - with
	  a
	  comment saying the internal header pulls in too much to include.
	  
	  Declaring them where the rest of the decoding interface lives
	  removes
	  that. The plotting structure stays an incomplete type: it is only
	  ever
	  passed by pointer, its layout is internal and moves with the
	  encoder, and
	  a caller that wires the hooks up never needs its fields. One that
	  does
	  need them includes the internal header and takes what comes with
	  it.
	  
	  The two now-redundant declarations are dropped.

