	  sound
	  interchangeable. Only "make check" can fail; "make test" prints a
	  count
	  of the bytes by which the encoder's output differs from the
	  committed
	  reference and always succeeds, and that count is expected to be
	  non-zero
	  on a different processor or compiler. Reaching for it to find out
	  whether
	  something is broken gives an answer that means nothing.

2026-07-27 11:19  aleidinger

	* Makefile.am, Makefile.in, doc/Doxyfile-internal.in,
	  doc/Makefile.am, doc/Makefile.in, doc/doxygen/internal.md,
	  doc/maintainer-abi.md, include/lame.def, maintainer/abi,
	  maintainer/abi/libmp3lame.abi, maintainer/abicheck.sh:
	  Add a "make abicheck" target for the exported library interface
	  
	  The exported interface is written down twice, once per platform,
	  and both
	  files are maintained by hand: the linker is told to export
	  exactly what
	  they list, so they are not documentation of the ABI, they are the
	  ABI.
	  Nothing checked them against each other or against the library
	  that was
	  actually built.
	  
	  The new target does, in three steps that get deeper as the tools
	  allow.
	  The export lists are compared with one another, which needs
	  nothing but
	  the source tree. The built library's export table is read back
	  and
	  compared with the list for its platform. Where libabigail is
	  installed
	  and the build carries debug information, the interface is
	  compared with a
	  baseline committed alongside, which also catches the changes that
	  keep
	  every name intact: a parameter that changed type, a struct that
	  gained a
	  member, an enum whose values moved.
	  
	  A missing tool skips a step and never fails the run; a step that
	  could be
	  made and did not hold does. The check needs no audio input and no
	  second
	  build to compare against, so it also runs from an unpacked
	  tarball, and
	  the baseline ships in the distribution so that it can.
	  
	  The two lists have never named the same set of symbols, and
	  bringing them
	  together is part of this. The rule they follow: one list per
	  operating
	  system, not one per build, each the logical OR over every
	  configuration
	  we ship. A symbol belongs in it when the code is compiled in and
	  is part
	  of the exported interface, whatever any single build does with
	  it.
	  Deprecated code that is still built is still exported and still
	  belongs;
	  only what is not compiled at all, or is not part of the
	  interface, stays
	  out.
	  
	  Applying that, the DLL gains what the shared library alone
	  exported so
	  far: the decoder analysis hooks, so that an analyzer frontend
	  outside the
	  library can reach them on Windows too (SF bug #515, which was
	  answered on
	  POSIX alone), the Latin-1 tag setters whose ucs2, utf16 and utf8
	  counterparts the DLL has exported all along, and the deprecated
	  entry
	  points whose declarations the header compiles out while their
	  definitions
	  are still built. Adding a name to the Windows list is additive;
	  nothing
	  that links against the DLL today is affected.
	  
	  What a green run does not say is written down with it: it
	  compares
	  against the union of the configurations we ship rather than the
	  one that
	  was built, so it cannot say what this build contains; it says
	  nothing
	  about behaviour, so an entry point that links and does nothing
	  passes; it
	  reads only the library built here, so a fault in the Windows list
	  surfaces only in a Windows build; and it inspects the library's
	  exports
	  rather than the programs we ship, so a symbol the frontend needs
	  can be
	  dropped with every check still green - which is how the analysis
	  hooks
	  were nearly lost.

