	* doc/Doxyfile-internal.in, doc/Makefile.am, doc/Makefile.in,
	  doc/doxygen/internal.md, doc/doxygen/vector-dispatch.dox,
	  libmp3lame/takehiro.c, libmp3lame/util.c:
	  Document the vector dispatch design decisions in the internal
	  docs
	  
	  The run-time SIMD dispatch for the hot quantization loops makes
	  two
	  choices that are counter-intuitive and easy to reopen later: the
	  ladder
	  skips SSE4.1/SSE4.2 entirely, and the variable-bitrate noise
	  estimate is
	  offered at SSE2 only. Add an internal Doxygen page recording why
	  - a
	  16-bit code path is denser than the 32-bit SSE4 instructions for
	  data
	  this small, and the noise routine is gather-bound with a fixed
	  reduction
	  order that a wider vector cannot exploit - and cross-reference it
	  from
	  the dispatch selector and the affected routines.

2026-07-25 10:35  aleidinger

	* Makefile.MSVC, libmp3lame/vbrquantize.c,
	  libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.am, libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.in,
	  libmp3lame/vector/lame_intrin.h,
	  libmp3lame/vector/xmm_calc_sfb_noise.c, test/unit/Makefile.am,
	  test/unit/Makefile.in, test/unit/test_calc_sfb_noise_vector.c,
	  vc_solution/vs_libmp3lame.vcxproj,
	  vc_solution/vs_libmp3lame.vcxproj.filters:
	  Vectorise the VBR scalefactor-band noise calculation
	  
	  Estimating how much quantization noise a scalefactor band would
	  carry is one
	  of the larger costs of a variable-bitrate encode: the band is
	  quantized and
	  the squared error summed, once per candidate scalefactor, many
	  times per
	  frame. As with the xr^(3/4) quantiser, every compiler was left to
	  vectorise
	  this on its own and they did so unevenly, so "the encoder" ran
	  materially
	  different code depending only on which one built it. The loop now
	  has an SSE2
	  implementation chosen at run time, the way the Huffman table
	  search and the
	  xr^(3/4) quantiser already are, so every x86-64 CPU runs it. An
	  AVX2 version
	  was written and measured and made no difference to this routine's
	  variable-bitrate work - the eight-wide gather is no faster than
	  the SSE2 one on
	  the cores tested - so it is not carried; the capability ladder
	  allows a routine
	  to exist at a single tier. The scalar routine stays as the common
	  ground and
	  the correctness reference, and narrow bands - about a third of
	  the calls,
	  carrying a fifth of the values - keep to it, below a width taken
	  from a
	  histogram of real encodes.
	  
	  The quantization part is elementwise and produces the same
	  integers as before.
	  The sum of squares is a floating-point reduction, and there the
	  compilers had
	  quietly disagreed: gcc and clang, under this library's fast-math,
	  reassociate
	  the sum into the natural horizontal one and agree with each
	  other, while MSVC
	  keeps the source order. The vector code pins the gcc/clang order
	  and holds the
	  running sum in a register so the compiler does not reassociate it
	  back, so the
	  vector code and all three toolchains now compute the estimate the
	  same
	  way where before it was compiler-dependent. Pinning that order
	  changes the
	  last bit of the estimate on the toolchains that had reassociated
	  differently.
	  On the material tested this leaves the encoded bitstream
	  unchanged on
	  everything except the hardest transient tracks, where it flips a
	  single
	  scalefactor; scored against the original with PEAQ that flip is
	  perceptually
	  identical (the objective difference grade does not move), and
	  every track of
	  the quality corpus scores an unchanged grade across the
	  variable-bitrate modes.
	  
	  Because the compilers already vectorised much of this, the wall
	  clock is
	  unchanged on gcc and clang; the measurable gain is on MSVC, which
	  had left more
	  of it scalar - a few percent of a variable-bitrate encode on a
	  Core i7-10700.
	  
	  A unit test checks the SSE2 routine against a double-precision
	  reference over
	  the tail cases and the ends of the index range, which a real
	  encode cannot be
	  relied on to reach. The scalar routine and the alternative
	  IEEE-754
	  formulation are left untouched; a build selecting the latter
	  keeps its own path.

2026-07-25 10:34  aleidinger

	* test/unit/Makefile.am, test/unit/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/test_choose_table_vector.c,
	  test/unit/test_encode_mono_interleaved.c,
	  test/unit/test_encode_nonfinite.c, test/unit/test_merge_argv.c,
	  test/unit/test_parse_pathlen.c, test/unit/test_sanity.c,
	  test/unit/test_totalframes.c, test/unit/test_unused.h:
	  Mark the unused unit-test parameter with the attribute macro
	  
	  Each cmocka test silences its unused fixture argument with a
	  `(void) state;`
	  statement in the body. The library marks a deliberately unused
	  parameter with
	  an attribute macro instead, and one of the tests had started to
	  follow that;
	  the rest had not, so the same intent was written two ways across
	  the suite.
	  
	  This settles on the macro everywhere the parameter is
	  unconditionally unused.
	  Most of these tests compile against the public library API alone
	  and pull in no
	  internal header; making them reach into one just to borrow the
	  macro would be
	  the wrong dependency for a public-API test, so they take it from
	  a small header
	  of their own that carries the same definition. The tests that
	  already include
	  the internal header use it directly.
	  
	  Only the always-unused case is converted. Casts that exist to
	  discard a return
	  value, and ones whose parameter is unused only in a particular
	  build
	  configuration, are deliberately left as they were - the macro
	  would say the
	  wrong thing about them.

2026-07-25 10:33  aleidinger

	* Makefile.MSVC, libmp3lame/takehiro.c,
	  libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.am, libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.in,
	  libmp3lame/vector/avx2_quantize_lines.c,
	  libmp3lame/vector/lame_intrin.h,
	  libmp3lame/vector/xmm_quantize_lines.c, test/unit/Makefile.am,
	  test/unit/Makefile.in, test/unit/test_quantize_lines_vector.c,
	  vc_solution/vs_libmp3lame.vcxproj,
	  vc_solution/vs_libmp3lame.vcxproj.filters:
	  Vectorise the quantization of xr^(3/4)
	  
	  Turning the scaled spectral values into quantized integers is one
	  of the
	  larger costs of a constant-bitrate encode, and every compiler was
	  left to
	  vectorise its inner loop or not on its own. Some do: current gcc
	  and clang
	  already emit the four-wide sequence. MSVC does not, and left the
	  loop
	  essentially scalar, so "the encoder" ran materially different
	  code depending
	  only on which compiler built it. Writing the loop out makes every
	  toolchain
	  run the same code, the way the CPU-dispatch ladder already makes
	  every CPU of
	  one architecture do - and it recovers about eight percent of a
	  constant-bitrate
	  encode on the toolchain that had been leaving it on the table,
	  with a gather on
	  the wider tier on top.
	  
	  The loop is elementwise - scale, truncate, look up a rounding
	  term, add,
	  truncate - with no accumulation, so the vector form produces the
	  same integers
	  as the scalar one and the output does not move. A unit test
	  checks it against
	  an independent scalar reference over the tail and the ends of the
	  index range,
	  which a real encode cannot be relied on to reach.
	  
	  There is one exception, and it is a property of the old code
	  rather than the
	  new. On 32-bit x86 the compilers compute scalar floating point in
	  the x87
	  unit, which keeps a wider intermediate than single precision; the
	  scalar loop
	  therefore rounded from that wider value, while the vector form
	  rounds from a
	  true single-precision one. So a 32-bit build's output shifts
	  slightly - the
	  same shift a 64-bit build already had relative to it, now
	  reaching 32-bit too.
	  64-bit builds, and 32-bit builds that do scalar float on SSE, are
	  unaffected.
	  
	  Short regions stay on the scalar path, where the setup does not
	  pay for
	  itself; a fifth of the calls are that short and they carry under
	  a percent of
	  the values.

2026-07-25 10:32  aleidinger

	* Makefile.MSVC, libmp3lame/Makefile.am, libmp3lame/Makefile.in,
	  libmp3lame/takehiro.c, libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.am,
	  libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.in,
	  libmp3lame/vector/avx2_choose_table.c,
	  libmp3lame/vector/lame_intrin.h,
	  libmp3lame/vector/xmm_choose_table.c,
	  libmp3lame/vector/xmm_quantize_sub.c, test/unit/Makefile.am,
	  test/unit/Makefile.in, test/unit/test_choose_table_vector.c,
	  vc_solution/vs_libmp3lame.vcxproj,
	  vc_solution/vs_libmp3lame.vcxproj.filters:
	  Vectorise the Huffman table search
	  
	  Choosing the table that encodes a region in the fewest bits is a
	  quarter to a
	  third of the time a constant-bitrate encode spends, and more than
	  a fifth of
	  a variable-bitrate one - the largest single cost in the encoder
	  that had no
	  vector implementation on any platform, and none of the compilers
	  in use
	  vectorise the table lookups on their own.
	  
	  The work is integer arithmetic over constant integer tables, so
	  the vector
	  form computes the same sums in a different order and the output
	  is identical
	  by construction rather than by measurement. A unit test checks
	  the routines
	  against a plain scalar reference over the boundaries an encode
	  cannot be
	  relied on to reach.
	  
	  What makes it worth doing is narrowing the values to sixteen bits
	  first.
	  They are quantized magnitudes far below that, and the narrower
	  form both puts
	  eight of them in a register instead of four and brings two useful
	  instructions into reach on the baseline: a signed maximum, and a
	  multiply-add
	  that turns a pair of values into their table index on its own. So
	  the whole
	  gain is available on any processor of the last two decades rather
	  than only
	  on recent ones; the wider tier adds a further doubling of the
	  maximum alone,
	  and nothing above the baseline helps the lookups at all.
	  
	  Narrowing saturates, which only the maximum can meet. It reports
	  its value
	  exactly while that value can matter, and something larger than
	  the rejection
	  threshold otherwise - which is all its caller asks. A
	  compile-time check
	  ties that to the threshold, so raising the threshold past the
	  saturation
	  point stops the build instead of quietly changing a result.
	  
	  Regions below a few dozen values stay on the scalar path: a third
	  of them are
	  that short, they hold about a twentieth of the values between
	  them, and the
	  vector form is slower there than the loop it replaces.

2026-07-25 10:31  aleidinger

	* ACM/ADbg/Makefile.in, ACM/Makefile.in, ACM/ddk/Makefile.in,
	  ACM/tinyxml/Makefile.in, Dll/Makefile.in, Makefile.in,
	  config.h.in, configMS.h, configure, configure.ac,
	  doc/Makefile.in, doc/html/Makefile.in, doc/html/detailed.html,
	  doc/man/Makefile.in, doc/man/lame.1, dshow/Makefile.in,
	  frontend/Makefile.in, frontend/parse.c, include/Makefile.in,
	  include/lame.h, libmp3lame/Makefile.in, libmp3lame/lame.c,
	  libmp3lame/lame_global_flags.h, libmp3lame/set_get.c,
	  libmp3lame/util.c, libmp3lame/util.h,
	  libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.in, mac/Makefile.in, misc/Makefile.in,
	  mpglib/Makefile.in, test/Makefile.in, test/unit/Makefile.in,
	  vc_solution/Makefile.in:
	  Detect AVX2 and offer it to the vector routines
	  
	  The encoder chooses its vector code from one ordered list of
	  instruction
	  sets, so adding a wider one is a new entry rather than another
	  flag tested at
	  every call site. AVX2 becomes the top entry, above SSE2: the
	  configure
	  summary reports what was built, and --verbose reports what the
	  running
	  machine actually got.
	  
	  The --noasm switch, which already turned the vector code off
	  wholesale, gains
	  a name for the new tier. It can disable AVX2 on its own and leave
	  SSE2 in
	  use, so one tier can be measured against the one below it and
	  against the
	  plain C path the way a speed test or a bug hunt wants; disabling
	  SSE still
	  removes the whole group above it, and the switch may be repeated
	  to turn off
	  more than one.
	  
	  Detecting AVX2 is not one question but three. The processor must
	  have the
	  instructions, the operating system must preserve the wider
	  registers across a
	  context switch, and only then may that state be read at all.
	  Asking the
	  first alone reports success on a system where the registers are
	  silently
	  corrupted, so the path that queries the processor directly now
	  asks all
	  three; compilers offering their own feature test already cover
	  it.
	  
	  The build-time test compiles both an integer and a floating point
	  construct,
	  because they do not answer alike: on 32-bit x86 under the
	  optimization flags
	  used here, one toolchain accepts the first and rejects the
	  second. A test
	  that asked about integers alone would promise a capability the
	  build cannot
	  deliver. Where the per-function form is rejected, the fallback
	  gives the
	  file its own baseline, as the tier below already does.
	  
	  The list is per architecture and names real instruction sets, so
	  each
	  architecture carries only the entries that exist on it and
	  reports its own.
	  None of it is exported and the library interface is unchanged.

