	* Dll/BladeMP3EncDLL.c, configMS.h:
	  Apply the Windows deprecation policy where it was not reaching
	  
	  The Windows configuration header turns off MSVC's deprecation
	  warnings
	  for the standard library names this code deliberately keeps, but
	  two
	  cases slipped past it. The legacy encoder DLL includes no
	  configuration
	  header at all, so nothing that header sets ever applied to it.
	  And the
	  POSIX names MSVC deprecates in favour of an underscored spelling
	  of its
	  own answer to a different switch than the one already set.
	  
	  Give the DLL the same configuration prologue every other source
	  has, and
	  set the second switch beside the first. The deprecation reports
	  left in
	  this build are the legacy sockets calls in the RTP frontend,
	  which are
	  worth replacing rather than silencing.

2026-07-25 10:29  aleidinger

	* configMS.h:
	  Take the fixed-width integer types from the standard header on
	  Windows
	  
	  The Windows configuration header declared int8_t and its siblings
	  itself, from a time before the compilers used here shipped
	  <stdint.h>.
	  Under MSVC that made int8_t an alias of plain char, so as soon as
	  an
	  optional library's header pulled in the real <stdint.h> the type
	  was
	  redefined as signed char and the build reported it. The
	  alternative
	  spelling in the same block declared the names as object-like
	  macros,
	  which silently corrupts any later declaration using one of them
	  as a
	  type.
	  
	  Include the standard header instead and report it as present, so
	  the
	  guards elsewhere in the tree that ask for it take the same path
	  on
	  Windows as they do everywhere else. The two floating-point
	  aliases the
	  block also declared had no users.

2026-07-25 10:28  aleidinger

	* libmp3lame/mpglib_interface.c:
	  Reject a decoded frame whose sample count does not fit the result
	  
	  The decoder returns the number of samples it produced in an int,
	  and the
	  demultiplexing loop indexes the caller's output buffers with it.
	  That
	  count was narrowed from the decoder's own size_t without a check.
	  libmpg123 bounds a single frame far below the limit, so this is
	  not
	  reachable in practice, but a wrapped count would then be used as
	  a
	  length against buffers sized by the caller.
	  
	  Report the failure at the point the count is computed instead.

2026-07-25 10:28  aleidinger

	* libmp3lame/set_get.c, test/unit/test_set_get.c:
	  Bound the maximum-samples estimate to the int it is returned in
	  
	  lame_get_maximum_number_of_samples() derives a sample count from
	  a
	  size_t buffer size and returns it in an int, without bounding any
	  step
	  of the estimate. From a buffer of about 780 MB upward the frame
	  count
	  multiplied by the frame size overflows, and the function returns
	  a
	  negative sample count, which no encode call can accept. Larger
	  buffers
	  return arbitrary values.
	  
	  This is not specific to 64-bit builds. Only the division narrows
	  with
	  the pointer width, and it is the least reachable of the three
	  conversions; the overflow of the product is reached on any
	  platform
	  whose size_t can express the buffer size.
	  
	  Bound each step to the range its result is returned in, so a
	  buffer too
	  large to describe reports the largest sample count an encode call
	  can
	  take rather than a wrapped one. Answers below that limit are
	  unchanged.

