	* ACM/ADbg/Makefile.in, ACM/Makefile.in, ACM/ddk/Makefile.in,
	  ACM/tinyxml/Makefile.in, Dll/BladeMP3EncDLL.c, Dll/Makefile.in,
	  Makefile.MSVC, Makefile.in, config.h.in, configMS.h, configure,
	  configure.ac, doc/Makefile.in, doc/html/Makefile.in,
	  doc/man/Makefile.in, dshow/Makefile.in, frontend/Makefile.in,
	  frontend/parse.c, include/Makefile.in, libmp3lame/Makefile.am,
	  libmp3lame/Makefile.in, libmp3lame/fft.c, libmp3lame/i386,
	  libmp3lame/lame.c, libmp3lame/quantize.c, libmp3lame/takehiro.c,
	  libmp3lame/util.c, libmp3lame/util.h,
	  libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.am, libmp3lame/vector/Makefile.in,
	  libmp3lame/vector/xmm_quantize_sub.c, mac/Makefile.in,
	  misc/Makefile.in, mpglib/Makefile.in, test/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/Makefile.in, vc_solution/Makefile.am,
	  vc_solution/Makefile.in, vc_solution/README.vs.txt,
	  vc_solution/vs_arch_nasm.props, vc_solution/vs_arch_sse2.props,
	  vc_solution/vs_arch_x87.props, vc_solution/vs_lame.slnx,
	  vc_solution/vs_lame_clients.slnx,
	  vc_solution/vs_lame_lame.vcxproj,
	  vc_solution/vs_lame_mp3rtp.vcxproj,
	  vc_solution/vs_lame_mp3x.vcxproj,
	  vc_solution/vs_libmp3lame.vcxproj,
	  vc_solution/vs_libmp3lame.vcxproj.filters,
	  vc_solution/vs_libmp3lame_dll.vcxproj, vc_solution/vs_nasm.props,
	  vc_solution/vs_nasm.targets, vc_solution/vs_nasm.xml:
	  Use the vector routines on every x86 build, and drop the assembly
	  they replace
	  
	  The vectorized routines have been part of LAME since 3.98, and
	  the two checks
	  that could switch them on only ever succeeded on 64-bit Windows:
	  one tested a
	  macro that every x86-64 Windows toolchain defines, the other was
	  defined in a
	  single Visual Studio property sheet. Even there only one of the
	  two routines
	  was reached; the other needed the property sheet. So Linux,
	  FreeBSD, macOS
	  and every 32-bit build compiled the routines into the binary and
	  then called
	  the plain C code instead.
	  
	  32-bit x86 did not even compile them. The configure check asked
	  whether the
	  compiler targets SSE2 by default, which is a stricter question
	  than the code
	  asks, since the routines carry a per-function target attribute
	  and need no
	  such baseline. That check was tightened in 3.100 to repair a real
	  build
	  failure, and the attribute that made the strictness unnecessary
	  arrived in
	  3.101, but the check was never revisited.
	  
	  Both conditions are replaced by what every toolchain already
	  publishes: a
	  compile-time test of the target baseline, and a run-time query of
	  the
	  processor where the baseline does not already guarantee the
	  instructions. The
	  routines are now built on every x86 target and chosen per CPU, so
	  one binary
	  serves machines with and without SSE2, and "lame --verbose"
	  reports what the
	  machine it is running on actually got. The configure check is
	  compiled with
	  the flags the build really uses, because one supported
	  combination rejects the
	  attribute and a check run with other flags would enable code that
	  then fails
	  to compile - which is how 32-bit x86 lost these routines in the
	  first place.
	  
	  With the intrinsics reaching every platform, the hand-written
	  assembler has
	  nothing left to offer. It required an external assembler, was
	  unreachable on
	  any 64-bit host, targeted an instruction set AMD removed from its
	  processors
	  in 2010, and half of its files had not been assembled by any
	  build system in
	  this century. It is removed, together with the build machinery,
	  the configure
	  switch and the Visual Studio configurations that existed to
	  select it or to
	  select the vector code that is now always present. No assembler
	  is needed to
	  build LAME any more.
	  
	  This changes which code path the encoder runs, so the output can
	  differ in the
	  last bits from a build that used the C path; it is
	  quality-neutral by
	  measurement rather than identical.

2026-07-22 12:51  aleidinger

	* Makefile.am, Makefile.in, doc/Doxyfile-internal.in,
	  doc/Makefile.am, doc/Makefile.in, doc/doxygen/internal.md,
	  doc/maintainer-coverage.md, maintainer/coverage-mkinputs.py,
	  maintainer/coverage-report.py, maintainer/coverage-run.sh,
	  maintainer/coverage-workload.txt,
	  maintainer/gen-coverage-matrix.sh:
	  Add a code coverage harness for the maintainer
	  
	  Sanitizer runs report only on code that actually executes, and
	  they are
	  far too slow to run against every option combination the encoder
	  accepts.
	  Choosing which invocations to run under them was a matter of
	  judgement,
	  with no way to tell an invocation that reaches new code from one
	  that only
	  repeats what another already covered.
	  
	  Measure it instead. The harness builds an instrumented matrix
	  whose cells
	  are the axes of conditional compilation - a line inside a
	  preprocessor
	  conditional that no configuration enables cannot be reached by
	  any test,
	  however the test is written - runs a workload against each cell
	  while
	  capturing coverage per invocation, and reports what each
	  configuration
	  uniquely contributes together with a ranked, minimal set of
	  invocations.
	  
	  The workload deliberately includes invocations that are supposed
	  to fail.
	  The input-rejection paths are reachable only by an invocation
	  that exits
	  non-zero, so a harness that treats a non-zero exit as its own
	  failure
	  silently drops exactly the error handling a sanitizer run most
	  wants to
	  exercise. Every entry declares its expected outcome and a
	  mismatch in
	  either direction is reported.
	  
	  The option lists under test/ are read directly rather than
	  copied, so the
	  encoder-settings coverage curated there stays maintained in one
	  place.
	  
	  Maintainer tooling; none of it is needed to build or use LAME.

