2026-07-22 11:10  aleidinger

	* frontend/get_audio.c:
	  Keep the careful-read sample counts at the width the API provides
	  
	  When told to read exactly as many samples as the input declares,
	  the
	  frontend held that declared count, and its running total of
	  samples
	  already read, in an unsigned int. The count reaches it from the
	  library
	  as an unsigned long. Where long is the wider type, a declared
	  length
	  above 2^32 samples was truncated before it was used, so reading
	  stopped
	  at the wrong point.
	  
	  Hold both at the source width instead. Only the final clamp still
	  narrows, and the test guarding it already bounds the value to
	  less than
	  one frame. Choosing the API's own type rather than a fixed-width
	  one
	  keeps this a no-op where long and int are the same width.
	  
	  The documented sentinel for an unknown length keeps its value at
	  either
	  width, so streams of unknown length are unaffected.
	  
	  SF patch #105 flagged the same conversion; it is addressed here
	  by
	  widening rather than by casting.

2026-07-22 11:09  aleidinger

	* frontend/get_audio.c, frontend/parse.c, frontend/timestatus.c:
	  Make the frontend's narrowing conversions to int explicit
	  
	  Where long is wider than int, the frontend implicitly narrows
	  long,
	  off_t and size_t values to int when storing sample counts,
	  decoder
	  delays, sample rates and argument lengths. Compilers that
	  diagnose
	  implicit narrowing warn at every one of them, so a warning-clean
	  frontend build is not possible on 64-bit targets.
	  
	  Cast at each site. Every value is bounded to the int range either
	  by
	  construction or by a range check already in place, so this states
	  the
	  intent of conversions that were already happening and does not
	  change
	  behaviour.
	  
	  Based on patches by Maya R. Odinezenko (SF patch #105, #106).

2026-07-22 11:09  aleidinger

	* libmp3lame/bitstream.c:
	  libmp3lame: avoid signed shift UB in the bitstream writer and CRC
	  
	  The MP3 writer assembles bytes by shifting a value into position
	  and OR-ing it
	  into the buffer. The value being placed can carry the sign bit,
	  so shifting it
	  left to position it is a left shift that overflows the signed int
	  - undefined,
	  though only the low bits are ever kept. The three writers that
	  share this
	  pattern (the two putbits variants and the header writer) now
	  shift through
	  unsigned, which is defined and leaves the stored bytes unchanged.
	  
	  The CRC-16 update likewise shifts its running value left each
	  step, and that
	  value grows past bit 30, making the signed shift a shift into the
	  sign bit.
	  Computing the CRC through unsigned locals makes every step
	  defined; the tested
	  bit and the polynomial XOR are unchanged, so the CRC is
	  identical.
	  
	  Output is bitstream-critical, so this was checked byte-for-byte
	  against the
	  unmodified library across steady-tone, transient/short-block, and
	  error-protected (CRC-bearing) encodes at several bitrate and
	  quality settings,
	  plus the decode paths - all identical. UBSan no longer reports
	  either line; the
	  baseline reproduced both, including the CRC shift, on the
	  error-protected
	  encode.

2026-07-22 11:08  aleidinger

	* libmp3lame/quantize.c:
	  libmp3lame: avoid signed shift UB in short-block scalefactor
	  amplification
	  
	  In the short-block subblock-gain path, a scalefactor is reduced
	  and, when it
	  goes negative, used to compute an amplification exponent: 210 +
	  (s << (scale +
	  1)). By that point s is always negative - the non-negative case
	  has already
	  continued - so the shift is a left shift of a negative value,
	  which UBSan
	  flags. The intent is s * 2^(scale + 1); s is a small negative
	  number that
	  cannot overflow, so writing it as a multiply by a power of two is
	  defined and
	  gives the identical result.
	  
	  Because this is quantization, output is bitstream-critical.
	  Verified
	  byte-identical against the unmodified library on transient,
	  short-block-heavy
	  material under -V2, -b 320, -q 0 and --preset insane (the
	  settings that reach
	  this path), as well as the steady-tone and tagged cases, and the
	  UBSan reports
	  for this line are gone (the baseline hit it six times on the
	  transient input).

2026-07-22 11:05  aleidinger

	* libmp3lame/id3tag.c:
	  libmp3lame: avoid signed shift UB in the ID3 frame-id matcher
	  
	  frame_id_matches walks a one-byte mask up through the four bytes
	  of a frame
	  id, shifting it left by 8 each step: 0xff, 0xff00, 0xff0000,
	  0xff000000. The
	  step that produces 0xff000000 overflows the signed int, and the
	  following one
	  shifts that negative value again - both undefined, and UBSan
	  flags the first.
	  The value is only ever used as a bitmask, so the sign is
	  irrelevant to the
	  result.
	  
	  Making the mask unsigned turns reaching and shifting past the top
	  byte into a
	  defined wrap and leaves every mask value, and therefore every
	  comparison and
	  the returned bits, exactly as before.
	  
	  Verified against the unmodified library: encoding with a full set
	  of ID3v2
	  tags (which is what drives this matcher) produces byte-identical
	  output, as do
	  the plain encode and decode paths, and the UBSan report for this
	  line is gone.

2026-07-22 11:04  aleidinger

	* frontend/get_audio.c:
	  frontend: avoid signed integer UB in the audio reader
	  
	  UBSan flags several integer operations in the WAV/AIFF/PCM reader
	  as
	  undefined, all value-dependent so a plain static pass cannot see
	  them. None
	  changes the output on a two's-complement machine, but each is
	  undefined by the
	  standard and worth making defined.
	  
	  unpack_read_samples assembles each PCM sample by shifting input
	  bytes into an
	  int. A byte of 0x80 or more shifted into the top position sets
	  the sign bit,
	  which is a left shift that overflows the signed int. The fix
	  shifts through
	  unsigned, which has the same width as int here (so every shift
	  amount stays in
	  range) and converts back to the same sample value. All eight
	  branches of the
	  byte-width/endianness ladder get the same treatment, not only the
	  three the
	  test corpus happened to reach, since they share the identical
	  defect.
	  
	  The MPEG decode path likewise shifts a 16-bit sample up into an
	  int; a negative
	  sample made that a left shift of a negative value, fixed the same
	  way.
	  
	  The WAV header size writer took a signed int. The streaming path
	  writes a
	  placeholder length of 0x7FFFFFFF, and the header adds the 44-byte
	  prefix to it,
	  overflowing the int. The size fields are unsigned by definition,
	  so the writer
	  now takes unsigned and the placeholder arithmetic is a defined
	  unsigned wrap -
	  the bytes emitted are unchanged.
	  
	  Verified value-identical against the unmodified reader:
	  8/16/32-bit WAV and
	  big-endian AIFF encode to byte-identical MP3, and decode to
	  byte-identical WAV
	  both to a file and to a pipe (the streaming placeholder path),
	  with the same
	  0x80000023 length still written. Frontend-only; the library and
	  the bitstream
	  are untouched.

2026-07-22 11:03  aleidinger

	* test/unit/Makefile.am, test/unit/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/test_set_get.c:
	  test: add a unit test for the libmp3lame parameter API
	  
	  set_get.c is almost all getter/setter pairs, and the command-line
	  frontend
	  drives only a fraction of them: a coverage run of the encoder
	  leaves more
	  than half the file's lines unexecuted. Those lines are not dead -
	  they are
	  the public interface every library caller relies on - so they are
	  exactly
	  the part that most needs a test and had none.
	  
	  The new test exercises each setter three ways where it applies: a
	  valid
	  round-trip through the matching getter, a call on an invalid
	  handle (the
	  validity-guard tail every function shares), and an out-of-range
	  value (the
	  reject path the frontend never takes). It groups the functions by
	  contract
	  - plain round-trip, 0/1 boolean validation, range checks,
	  clamping, and
	  setters with side effects on other fields - and adds the
	  read-only,
	  init-populated getters and the surviving deprecated stubs. A few
	  of those
	  stubs are still exported but no longer prototyped in the public
	  header; the
	  test declares them locally so that slice of the ABI is covered
	  too.
	  
	  The internal-only tuning setters (masking adjust, ATH curve,
	  short-block
	  thresholds, substep, sfscale, subblock gain, and the like) are
	  covered
	  under the very gate the frontend uses for them: a normal build
	  compiles
	  that section out, while a build configured with --enable-internal
	  runs the
	  extra checks against the private set_get.h. Those symbols are
	  withheld from
	  the shared library, so under that option the test links the
	  static archive
	  to reach them. This half depends on the internal-options build
	  support, so
	  it must be applied after that change.
	  
	  This lifts set_get.c line coverage from roughly one half to 93%
	  on its own,
	  and gives the ABI a regression guard: a change that alters a
	  validation
	  rule, a clamp boundary, or an invalid-handle return now fails a
	  test
	  instead of silently shifting behaviour for downstream callers.

2026-07-22 11:02  aleidinger

	* configure, configure.ac, frontend/Makefile.am,
	  frontend/Makefile.in, libmp3lame/set_get.h:
	  build: repair the alpha internal-options build
	  
	  The frontend gates its developer-only tuning options (short-block
	  thresholds,
	  masking adjust, ATH curve, substep, sfscale, subblock gain, tune,
	  preset-notune) behind a switch that, until now, nothing turned
	  on: it was tied
	  to no configure option and not to the alpha version either. So
	  even in an alpha
	  release those options parsed and silently did nothing. Worse, if
	  the switch was
	  turned on by hand the frontend no longer linked at all, because
	  the setters it
	  then calls are deliberately absent from the shared library's
	  export list.
	  
	  This adds a configure option, --enable-internal, that makes the
	  feature real
	  and buildable. It is only meaningful for an alpha version, so
	  asking for it on a
	  beta or release tree is a hard configure error rather than a
	  silent no-op.
	  Because the internal setters are intentionally not exported, a
	  frontend that
	  uses them must link the static archive; --enable-internal
	  therefore forces
	  static frontend linking, and configure reports that it did so,
	  and why,
	  alongside a short summary of the resulting configuration. The
	  export list is
	  left untouched: a build with the option enabled produces a shared
	  library with
	  exactly the same public interface as one without it.
	  
	  The switch is defined per target - for the frontend and for the
	  parameter-API
	  unit test that also exercises these setters - rather than
	  globally. A global
	  definition would drag the internal code path of the argument
	  parser into the
	  other unit tests that compile it only for their argument helpers;
	  those link
	  the shared library, where the setters are not present. Keeping
	  the definition
	  local leaves them, and the library itself, exactly as they were.
	  
	  The internal header follows suit: the prototypes for these
	  setters are now
	  visible only when the option is enabled, matching the frontend.
	  The handful
	  that the library calls internally (the preset entry point, the
	  scalefactor-scale
	  setter, the M/S fix setter) stay visible in every build, since
	  intra-library
	  callers need them regardless.

2026-07-22 11:02  aleidinger

	* frontend/get_audio.c:
	  Scale IEEE-float samples by a single full-scale factor
	  
	  The float sample reader scaled positive and negative samples by
	  two
	  constants named after the largest and the smallest representable
	  integer. Neither name described its value: the negative one was
	  negated
	  into a positive magnitude, and the positive one was converted
	  from a
	  number a float cannot hold and rounded up. Both therefore stood
	  for
	  2^31, so the distinction the names implied did not exist.
	  
	  Use one constant named for what it is, the magnitude a full-scale
	  sample
	  maps to, and record why the surrounding clamps keep the
	  conversion in
	  range. The value is unchanged, so float input encodes
	  byte-identically.

