	  has no
	  definition and no callers; it carried the same stale bound.

2026-07-19 11:49  aleidinger

	* frontend/main.c:
	  Expand long filenames correctly on Windows
	  
	  Expanding the input path from its short 8.3 form copied the long
	  name
	  with a bound derived from the remaining buffer space rather than
	  from the
	  name, and neither branch guaranteed a terminating NUL. Where the
	  path had
	  no directory part this left the tail of the previous, longer name
	  in
	  place, and a name that did not fit overwrote the path with a
	  truncated
	  one instead of being rejected.
	  
	  Measure the name, establish where it goes, and copy it with a
	  bounded
	  conversion that always terminates, leaving the path untouched
	  when the
	  result would not fit. The search for the last separator no longer
	  subtracts pointers that may be null.

2026-07-19 11:46  aleidinger

	* frontend/get_audio.c, frontend/get_audio.h, frontend/lame_main.c:
	  Report output write failures when decoding to a file
	  
	  The decode path wrote the WAV header and the decoded samples
	  without
	  checking whether the writes succeeded, so a failure such as a
	  full
	  output device was ignored: the whole input was decoded into a
	  stream
	  that could accept nothing, and the program still exited
	  successfully
	  leaving a truncated or empty file.
	  
	  Let the sample writer report a failed write and stop the decode
	  at the
	  first one, reporting the error and exiting with a non-zero
	  status.
	  Successful decodes are unaffected.

2026-07-19 11:41  aleidinger

	* libmp3lame/VbrTag.c:
	  Avoid undefined signed left-shift when masking LAME-tag header
	  fields
	  
	  The bit-packing that assembles the LAME/Info tag frame header
	  formed its
	  low-bit field mask by complementing a left-shifted signed -1. A
	  left
	  shift of a negative value is undefined behaviour in C, and every
	  use of
	  the macro was flagged by -Wshift-negative-value.
	  
	  Form the mask from an unsigned all-ones value instead. The mask
	  value is
	  unchanged, so the emitted tag - and thus the whole encoded
	  bitstream -
	  is byte-identical; verified across VBR, CBR and ABR encodes.

