	  Decoding has gone through libmpg123 since 3.101, and the older
	  bundled
	  mpglib and Amiga MPEGA decoder paths are no longer selected by
	  any build.
	  Remove the dead code they left: the mpglib header scan and frame
	  reader
	  in the frontend, the Amiga decoder source, the stale build flags
	  and
	  header comments, and the legacy hand-written Unix makefile that
	  still
	  enabled them.
	  
	  The --mp1input, --mp2input and --mp3input options depend on the
	  decoder,
	  so offer them only when it is built in; they were previously
	  listed under
	  the removed bundled-decoder macro and so never shown at all.
	  Remove the
	  long-deprecated --ogginput option, which only printed a notice.
	  
	  The public decode interface stays; it is implemented on top of
	  libmpg123
	  and keeps working, and libmp3lame's exported symbols are
	  unchanged.

2026-07-17 18:10  aleidinger

	* libmp3lame/lame.c, test/unit/Makefile.am, test/unit/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/test_encode_mono_interleaved.c:
	  libmp3lame: reject an interleaved encode on a mono session
	  
	  The interleaved encoding entry points read the left and right
	  channels
	  from one buffer with a stride of two. On a session opened with a
	  single
	  input channel that buffer holds only one channel, and nothing
	  bounds the
	  strided read, so the second channel is read a channel's worth of
	  samples
	  past the end of the buffer.
	  
	  Refuse the combination with an input-data error rather than read
	  outside
	  the caller's buffer. A mono session is fed through the
	  non-interleaved
	  entry points, which is what the interleaving front ends already
	  do.
	  
	  The unit tests cover each interleaved entry point on a mono
	  session, and
	  check that an ordinary mono encode and a stereo interleaved
	  encode are
	  left working.
	  
	  Reported on SourceForge (bug #522).

2026-07-17 12:59  aleidinger

	* doc/html/history.html:
	  doc: add changelog entries for the noise shaping and termcap
	  fixes

2026-07-17 12:58  aleidinger

	* configure, configure.ac:
	  Detect the terminal library by the interface the console code
	  uses
	  
	  The probe asked each candidate library for initscr, which nothing
	  in LAME
	  calls. The console code drives the terminal through the termcap
	  interface,
	  tgetent, tgetnum and tgetstr, and selects its header from
	  whichever one is
	  present. A library can offer initscr and still fail to satisfy
	  that header:
	  where ncurses is packaged as a static library whose termcap
	  header declares
	  those entry points as DLL imports, the probe succeeded and the
	  frontend
	  then failed to link against the library the probe had picked.
	  
	  Compiling the same header and calls the console code compiles
	  asks the
	  question the link step will ask. The candidates are also tried in
	  the order
	  they are written, keeping the first that satisfies the test
	  rather than the
	  last to be tested, which is what the unguarded checks amounted
	  to.
	  
	  Where none of them satisfies it, terminal support stays disabled,
	  a case
	  the console code already accounts for, instead of breaking the
	  build.
	  
	  SourceForge bug #436.

2026-07-17 12:57  aleidinger

	* USAGE, doc/html/detailed.html, doc/man/lame.1, libmp3lame/lame.c:
	  libmp3lame: disable amplified noise shaping for CBR and ABR
	  
	  Amplified noise shaping degrades CBR and ABR encoding instead of
	  improving it, which is why the faster quality settings, the ones
	  that
	  leave it off, sound better than the slower ones. It applies to
	  the
	  default quality setting.
	  
	  The amplification weighs bands against a distortion measure
	  produced by
	  the psychoacoustic model. CBR and ABR were moved onto the newer
	  model
	  without adapting their encoding loops to it, so that measure no
	  longer
	  means to them what the amplification assumes. VBR was written
	  against
	  that model and is unaffected, so it keeps the amplification.
	  
	  Keeping the amplification out of the two modes it hurts treats
	  the
	  symptom; reconciling those encoding loops with the psychoacoustic
	  model
	  is still to be done.
	  
	  For these two modes the amplification was the only difference
	  between
	  quality settings 3 and 4, and between 1 and 2, so each of those
	  pairs
	  selects the same encoding.
	  
	  Reported and analysed by Maik Merten (SF bug #516).

