	* include/lame.h, libmp3lame/lame.c, test/unit/Makefile.am,
	  test/unit/Makefile.in, test/unit/test_encode_nonfinite.c:
	  Reject PCM input which is not a finite number
	  
	  A NaN or an infinity has no meaning as an audio sample, but the
	  floating point
	  entry points pass whatever they are given into the psycho
	  acoustic model, where
	  it spreads across the band energies and turns the surrounding
	  frames into noise.
	  A debug build then aborts deep inside the model, far away from
	  the buffer which
	  caused it, and a release build encodes the result without a word.
	  Callers
	  computing their samples rather than reading them from a file - a
	  resampler
	  dividing by zero, a decoder passing on a corrupt stream - can
	  produce such
	  values without noticing.
	  
	  Screen the floating point sample types while the data can still
	  be attributed
	  to the caller's buffer, and refuse the call with an error code of
	  its own,
	  which leaves the ABI intact. Integer sample types cannot express
	  a non-finite
	  value and are taken as given, so those entry points are untouched
	  and the
	  encoded bitstream does not change. Refusal is an ordinary
	  documented outcome,
	  so it returns rather than asserting.
	  
	  The screening inspects the exponent bits instead of calling
	  isfinite(): the
	  library is built with fast floating point maths, under which the
	  compiler may
	  assume that no such value occurs and fold the check away. It
	  costs about a
	  tenth of a percent of the encoding time.
	  
	  The unit tests also pin the input ranges the entry points
	  document, which a
	  suite built only from NaNs could not tell apart from a screen
	  which rejected
	  everything.

