2026-07-14 18:21  aleidinger

	* INSTALL, Makefile.MSVC, config.h.in, configure, configure.ac,
	  doc/html/history.html, doc/maintainer-build-matrix.md,
	  maintainer/gen-build-matrix.sh,
	  vc_solution/vs2026_lame_config.props:
	  Detect and enable optimization and hardening flags across the
	  builds
	  
	  The Unix build chose optimization flags from hard-coded,
	  per-compiler-version
	  and per-CPU lists that had decayed over time: flags dropped from
	  current GCC,
	  a Clang path that never enabled -ffast-math, and -mtune=native
	  leaking into
	  generic i586/i686 builds. Probe the compiler at configure time
	  and keep only
	  the flags it accepts, so GCC and Clang each get a suitable set
	  without upkeep
	  as they evolve. The same probing drives --enable-expopt, and
	  --enable-native
	  offers machine-specific tuning as an explicit, non-portable
	  opt-in.
	  
	  Enable security hardening by default (fortified libc calls,
	  stack-protector-strong, stack-clash and control-flow protection,
	  full RELRO
	  and a non-executable stack); --disable-hardening opts out. Link
	  the frontends
	  dynamically by default so they can be built as
	  position-independent
	  executables; --disable-dynamic-frontends links a self-contained
	  static
	  frontend. Give the Visual Studio / MSVC build the matching
	  hardening (Control
	  Flow Guard, buffer security checks, CET, DEP, ASLR), drop several
	  obsolete
	  code-generation switches, and compile the release build's
	  consistency
	  assertions out.
	  
	  Per-component encoding speed on two machines (synthetic input,
	  single core,
	  medians of 12 runs; not comparable between machines, and
	  system-dependent):
	  
	  configuration step GCC / i7-10700 Clang / Xeon X5675
	  VBR CBR VBR CBR
	  new opt + hardening, static +0.1% -0.3% -7.2% -5.9% vs old
	  baseline
	  dynamic / PIE default +1.7% +0.6% +3.3% +4.6% vs static above
	  --enable-expopt=norm +2.1% +3.1% -0.2% -3.5% vs default
	  --enable-native -8.5% -8.8% -1.1% -5.0% vs default
	  net: default vs old baseline +1.9% +0.2% -4.1% -1.6%
	  
	  (Negative is faster.) The default is faster on Clang, which picks
	  up
	  -ffast-math, and about a percent slower on GCC, the cost of
	  position-
	  independent frontends. --enable-expopt=norm is not a win for this
	  workload;
	  --enable-native helps most on recent CPUs.
	  
	  The Windows builds have no configure step, so their flags are
	  fixed. A 32-bit
	  MSVC command-line build (i7-10700, medians of 9 runs) is about
	  3.7% faster VBR
	  and 2.5% faster CBR overall: dropping the stale switches and the
	  release
	  assertions saves roughly 4.7% / 3.0%, against which the hardening
	  options cost
	  about a percent. Fast floating point is not enabled on Windows;
	  on a 32-bit
	  build it perturbs the psychoacoustic model past the tolerance its
	  assertions
	  allow.

