	* doc/Doxyfile-common.in, doc/Doxyfile.in, frontend/parse.c,
	  test/unit/Makefile.am, test/unit/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/test_merge_argv.c:
	  frontend: size the merged argument vector to fit instead of a
	  fixed 512
	  
	  parse_args() merged the LAMEOPT tokens and the real argv[] into a
	  fixed
	  str_argv[512]. merge_argv() correctly skipped writes past slot
	  512, but it
	  returned the raw, unclamped argc + str_argc - 1 - so with more
	  than ~511
	  total tokens the caller looped past the initialised entries and
	  dereferenced
	  uninitialised str_argv[] slots as argument pointers (a stack
	  over-read from
	  an ordinary but long command line), and every argument past the
	  512th was
	  silently dropped.
	  
	  Allocate str_argv to actually hold every token (argc + the
	  LAMEOPT length as
	  an upper bound on its token count + 1) and free it when done;
	  argc and the
	  environment are both bounded by the OS ARG_MAX, so the size stays
	  in range.
	  This removes both the over-read and the silent truncation.
	  merge_argv() also
	  clamps its returned count to N as a defensive invariant, so the
	  helper can
	  never report more entries than the array it was given holds.

2026-07-12 06:45  aleidinger

	* frontend/parse.c, test/unit/Makefile.am, test/unit/Makefile.in,
	  test/unit/parse_test_stubs.c, test/unit/test_parse_pathlen.c:
	  frontend: bound positional input/output filename length
	  
	  The positional filename arguments (input, output, and each nogap
	  input)
	  were copied into their char[PATH_MAX+1] buffers with
	  strncpy(dst, argv[i], PATH_MAX+1). strncpy only null-terminates
	  when the
	  source is shorter than the count, so a filename of PATH_MAX+1
	  bytes or
	  more filled the buffer completely with no terminator; scanPath()
	  and
	  scanBasename() then scan it as a plain C string ("for (; *s;
	  ++s)") and
	  walk off the end of the stack array.
	  
	  The long-option paths (--nogapout, --out-dir) already guard
	  against this
	  by checking strnlen(nextArg, PATH_MAX) and force-terminating.
	  Factor that
	  bounded copy into a small helper, set_path_arg(), and route all
	  three
	  positional-filename copies through it: an over-long name is
	  rejected with
	  an error instead of leaving a non-terminated buffer.

